"markdown": "[_\ue80a_](javascript:void\\(0\\))\nContact\/Tip Us\n[ _\uf0e0_ Reach out to get featured\u2014contact us to send your exclusive story idea, research, hacks, or ask us a question or leave a comment\/feedback! ](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/p\/submit-news.html)\nFollow Us On Social Media\n[_\uf167_](https:\/\/www.youtube.com\/c\/thehackernews?sub_confirmation=1)\n[_\uf09e_ RSS Feeds](https:\/\/feeds.feedburner.com\/TheHackersNews) [_\uf0f3_ Email Alerts](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/asus-patches-driverhub-rce-flaws.html#email-outer) [_\uf2c6_ Telegram Channel](https:\/\/t.me\/+wKrQxniFO1Q1OTRk)\n[![Zenith Live 2025](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEiKLqdTf2BnL4fIex_Fj-HhjsQU6HOZ_ao_WyUZ0ELrxsZ4ezufQqD8JfJabnIMeCtgdwKU-y948oMzHGFOP_Ox3vMW1Ymki-zmY4bSHWFP-m4zwD42-Huw3q9eM8VAY9_mL9yzihdsF1YygoUH-dDrY8EzKJb4dslf03xMaGFlYgAy0bA6p7gZ5vr5GHHb\/s728-e300\/team-d.jpg)](https:\/\/thehackernews.uk\/team-d)\n# [ASUS Patches DriverHub RCE Flaws Exploitable via HTTP and Crafted .ini Files](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/asus-patches-driverhub-rce-flaws.html)\n_\ue802_ May 12, 2025 _\ue804_ Ravie LakshmananVulnerability \/ Endpoint Security\n[![ASUS Patches DriverHub RCE Flaws](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEhMHSERvz_B4vcYvDtFx9vunRDey58NlkfXaLkCzMqXhuMFflyGL1qlvbHXspuSkWbizefW_yCsuZcvZvBPCsyNt1_6TnvQNFoubIqaqXa7nZsIeViniO2b-xiz_ryD3r6IaPgo9W27J9uW5OmJKuKc9U_2wAHvVaRSfrKZOuFrypEwQdJDS2jlCm0nzfCQ\/s728-rw-e365\/asus.jpg)](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEhMHSERvz_B4vcYvDtFx9vunRDey58NlkfXaLkCzMqXhuMFflyGL1qlvbHXspuSkWbizefW_yCsuZcvZvBPCsyNt1_6TnvQNFoubIqaqXa7nZsIeViniO2b-xiz_ryD3r6IaPgo9W27J9uW5OmJKuKc9U_2wAHvVaRSfrKZOuFrypEwQdJDS2jlCm0nzfCQ\/s728-rw-e365\/asus.jpg)\nASUS has released updates to address two security flaws impacting ASUS DriverHub that, if successfully exploited, could enable an attacker to leverage the software in order to achieve remote code execution.\n[DriverHub](https:\/\/www.asus.com\/support\/faq\/1053934\/) is a tool that's designed to automatically detect the motherboard model of a computer and display necessary driver updates for subsequent installation by communicating with a [dedicated site](https:\/\/driverhub.asus.com\/en) hosted at \"driverhub.asus[.]com.\"\nThe flaws identified in the software are listed below -\n  * **[CVE-2025-3462](https:\/\/www.cve.org\/CVERecord?id=CVE-2025-3462)** (CVSS score: 8.4) - An origin validation error vulnerability that may allow unauthorized sources to interact with the software's features via crafted HTTP requests\n  * **[CVE-2025-3463](https:\/\/www.cve.org\/CVERecord?id=CVE-2025-3463)** (CVSS score: 9.4) - An improper certificate validation vulnerability that may allow untrusted sources to affect system behavior via crafted HTTP requests\n\n\nSecurity researcher MrBruh, who is credited with discovering and reporting the two vulnerabilities, said they could be exploited to achieve remote code execution as part of a one-click attack.\n[![Cybersecurity](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/asus-patches-driverhub-rce-flaws.html)](https:\/\/thehackernews.uk\/drata-d-3 \"Cybersecurity\")\nThe attack chain essentially involves tricking an unsuspecting user into visiting a sub-domain of driverhub.asus[.]com (e.g., driverhub.asus.com.<random string>.com) and then leveraging the DriverHub's UpdateApp endpoint to execute a legitimate version of the \"AsusSetup.exe\" binary with an option set to run any file hosted on the fake domain.\n\"When executing AsusSetup.exe it first reads from AsusSetup.ini, which contains metadata about the driver,\" the researcher [explained](https:\/\/mrbruh.com\/asusdriverhub\/) in a technical report.\n\"If you run AsusSetup.exe with the -s flag (DriverHub calls it using this to do a silent install), it will execute whatever is specified in SilentInstallRun. In this case, the ini file specifies a cmd script that performs an automated headless install of the driver, but it could run anything.\"\nAll an attacker needs to successfully pull off the exploit is to create a domain, and host three files, the malicious payload to be run, an altered version of AsusSetup.ini that has the \"SilentInstallRun\" property set to the malicious binary, and AsusSetup.exe, which then make use of the property to run the payload.\nFollowing responsible disclosure on April 8, 2025, the issues were fixed by ASUS on May 9. There is no evidence that the vulnerabilities have been exploited in the wild.\n\"This update includes important security updates and ASUS strongly recommends that users update their ASUS DriverHub installation to the latest version,\" the company [said](https:\/\/www.asus.com\/content\/asus-product-security-advisory\/) in a bulletin. \"The latest Software Update can be accessed by opening ASUS DriverHub, then clicking the 'Update Now' button.\"\nFound this article interesting? Follow us on and to read more exclusive content we post.\nSHARE [_\uf1e0_](javascript:void\\(0\\))\n_\uf1e0_ Share\n[_\ue80a_](javascript:void\\(0\\)) [_\uf1d4_ Share on Hacker News](https:\/\/news.ycombinator.com\/submitlink?u=https%3A%2F%2Fthehackernews.com%2F2025%2F05%2Fasus-patches-driverhub-rce-flaws.html&t=ASUS%20Patches%20DriverHub%20RCE%20Flaws%20Exploitable%20via%20HTTP%20and%20Crafted%20.ini%20Files) _\uf0e0_ Share on Email [_\uf232_ Share on WhatsApp](https:\/\/api.whatsapp.com\/send?text=ASUS%20Patches%20DriverHub%20RCE%20Flaws%20Exploitable%20via%20HTTP%20and%20Crafted%20.ini%20Files \u2014 https%3A%2F%2Fthehackernews.com%2F2025%2F05%2Fasus-patches-driverhub-rce-flaws.html) [![Facebook Messenger](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/asus-patches-driverhub-rce-flaws.html)Share on Facebook Messenger](fb-messenger:\/\/share\/?link=https%3A%2F%2Fthehackernews.com%2F2025%2F05%2Fasus-patches-driverhub-rce-flaws.html&app_id=280117418781535) [_\uf2c6_ Share on Telegram](https:\/\/telegram.me\/share\/url?url=https%3A%