"title": "Fake KeePass password manager leads to ESXi ransomware attack",,
"url": "https:\/\/www.bleepingcomputer.com\/news\/security\/fake-keepass-password-manager-leads-to-esxi-ransomware-attack\/",,
"summary": "The article describes a cybersecurity threat in which attackers have been distributing trojanized versions of the KeePass password manager. This malicious campaign, discovered by WithSecure's Threat Intelligence team, leveraged KeeLoader, a trojanized version of KeePass, installed via fake software promoted through Bing advertisements. KeeLoader includes a Cobalt Strike beacon and can export KeePass password databases, which are then stolen. Linked to the Black Basta ransomware group, the threat actors used a watermark in the Cobalt Strike beacon. The campaign involved typo-squatting domains to distribute the malware, and ultimately led to a VMware ESXi servers being encrypted. The group responsible, UNC4696, has been linked to previous Nitrogen Loader campaigns.",,
