"markdown": "[_\ue80a_](javascript:void\\(0\\))\nContact\/Tip Us\n[ _\uf0e0_ Reach out to get featured\u2014contact us to send your exclusive story idea, research, hacks, or ask us a question or leave a comment\/feedback! ](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/p\/submit-news.html)\nFollow Us On Social Media\n[_\uf167_](https:\/\/www.youtube.com\/c\/thehackernews?sub_confirmation=1)\n[_\uf09e_ RSS Feeds](https:\/\/feeds.feedburner.com\/TheHackersNews) [_\uf0f3_ Email Alerts](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/go-based-malware-deploys-xmrig-miner-on.html#email-outer) [_\uf2c6_ Telegram Channel](https:\/\/t.me\/+wKrQxniFO1Q1OTRk)\n[![Zenith Live 2025](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEiKLqdTf2BnL4fIex_Fj-HhjsQU6HOZ_ao_WyUZ0ELrxsZ4ezufQqD8JfJabnIMeCtgdwKU-y948oMzHGFOP_Ox3vMW1Ymki-zmY4bSHWFP-m4zwD42-Huw3q9eM8VAY9_mL9yzihdsF1YygoUH-dDrY8EzKJb4dslf03xMaGFlYgAy0bA6p7gZ5vr5GHHb\/s728-e300\/team-d.jpg)](https:\/\/thehackernews.uk\/team-d)\n# [Go-Based Malware Deploys XMRig Miner on Linux Hosts via Redis Configuration Abuse](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/go-based-malware-deploys-xmrig-miner-on.html)\n_\ue802_ May 20, 2025 _\ue804_ Ravie LakshmananLinux \/ Cryptojacking\n[![Go-Based Malware](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/go-based-malware-deploys-xmrig-miner-on.html)](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEgyrrjRLyS__q1T8oDRP47Ju4U2o72Lwh2MFGZq6PtZGiPVMs4_oML7zQhFl-CbQ1qHxwaP6ouYISZsNhNa6p0SI3Oxpe4_DgJyxH8ZSODwCFem6nKaPs9JpNj2wuTd30NNyuLuYLvhedyHfW_uGBXzCZP4zlwYS_KLK8WLlxILnCv4AuRaLtmuy0CssviL\/s728-rw-e365\/linux-crypto-malware.jpg)\nCybersecurity researchers are calling attention to a new Linux cryptojacking campaign that's targeting publicly accessible Redis servers.\nThe malicious activity has been codenamed **RedisRaider** by Datadog Security Labs.\n\"RedisRaider aggressively scans randomized portions of the IPv4 space and uses legitimate Redis configuration commands to execute malicious cron jobs on vulnerable systems,\" security researchers Matt Muir and Frederic Baguelin [said](https:\/\/securitylabs.datadoghq.com\/articles\/redisraider-weaponizing-misconfigured-redis\/).\nThe end goal of the campaign is to drop a Go-based primary payload that's responsible for unleashing an XMRig miner on compromised systems.\nThe activity entails using a bespoke scanner to identify publicly accessible Redis servers across the internet and then issuing an INFO command to determine if the instances are running on a Linux host. If it's found to be the case, the scanning algorithm proceeds to abuse Redis's SET command to inject a cron job.\n[![Cybersecurity](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/go-based-malware-deploys-xmrig-miner-on.html)](https:\/\/thehackernews.uk\/drata-d-3 \"Cybersecurity\")\nThe malware then uses the CONFIG command to change the Redis working directory to \"\/etc\/cron.d\" and write to the location a [database file](https:\/\/redis.io\/learn\/operate\/redis-at-scale\/persistence-and-durability\/persistence-options-in-redis) named \"apache\" so that it's periodically picked by the cron scheduler and runs a Base64-encoded shell script, which subsequently downloads the RedisRaider binary from a remote server.\nThe payload essentially serves as a dropper for a bespoke version of XMRig and also propagates the malware to other Redis instances, effectively expanding its reach and scale.\n\"In addition to server-side cryptojacking, RedisRaider's infrastructure also hosted a web-based Monero miner, enabling a multi-pronged revenue generation strategy,\" the researchers said.\n\"The campaign incorporates subtle anti-forensics measures, such as short-key time-to-live (TTL) settings and database configuration changes, to minimize detection and hinder post-incident analysis.\"\nThe disclosure comes as Guardz disclosed details of a targeted campaign exploiting legacy authentication protocols in Microsoft Entra ID to brute-force accounts. The activity, observed between March 18 and April 7, 2025, has been found to leverage [BAV2ROPC](https:\/\/redcanary.com\/blog\/threat-detection\/bav2ropc\/) (short for \"Basic Authentication Version 2 - Resource Owner Password Credential\") to bypass defenses like multi-factor authentication (MFA) and Conditional Access.\n\"The tracking and investigation revealed systematic exploitation attempts that leveraged BAV2ROPC's inherent design limitations, which predated contemporary security architectures,\" Elli Shlomo, head of security research at Guardz, [said](https:\/\/guardz.com\/blog\/the-legacy-loophole-how-attackers-are-exploiting-entra-id-and-what-to-do-about-it\/). \"The threat actors behind this campaign showed a deep understanding of identity systems.\"\nThe attacks are said to have originated mainly from Eastern Europe and the Asia-Pacific regions, primarily targeting admin accounts using legacy authentication endpoints.\n\"While regular users received the bulk of authentication attempts (50,214), admin accounts and shared mailboxes were targeted at a specific pattern, with admin accounts receiving 9,847 attempts across 432 IPs over 8 hours, suggesting an average of 22.79 attempts per IP and a velocity of 1,230.87 attempts per hour,\" the company said.\n[![Cybersecurity](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/go-based-malware-deploys-xmrig-miner-on.html)](https:\/\/thehackernews.uk\/tines-incident-response-d \"Cybersecurity\")\n\"This indicates a highly automated and concentrated attack campaign specifically designed to compromise privileged accounts while maintaining a broader attack surface against regular users.\"\nThis is not the first time legacy protocols have been abused for malicious activities. In 2021, Microsoft [divulged](https:\/\/www.microsoft.com\/en-us\/security\/blog\/2021\/06\/14\/behind-the-scenes-of-business-email-compromise-using-cross-domain-threat-data-to-disrupt-a-large-bec-infrastructure\/) a large-scale business email compromise (BEC) campaign that used BAV2ROPC and IMAP\/POP3 to circumvent MFA and exfiltrate email data.\nTo mitigate the risks posed by such attacks, it's advised to block legacy authentication via a Conditional Access policy, disable BAV2ROPC, and turn off SMTP AUTH in Exchange Online if not in use.\nFound this article interesting? Follow us on and to read more exclusive content we post.\nSHARE [_\uf09a_](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/go-based-malware-deploys-xmrig-miner-on.html#link_share) [_\uf099_](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/go-based-malware-deploys-xmrig-miner-on.html#link_share) [_\uf0e1_](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/go-based-malware-deploys-xmrig-miner-on.html#link_share) [_\uf1e0_](javascript:void\\(0\\))\n[_\uf099_ Tweet](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/go-based-malware-deploys-xmrig-miner-on.html#link_share)\n[_\uf0e1_ Share](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/go-based-malware-deploys-xmrig-miner-on.html#link_share)\n[_\uf09a_ Share](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/go-based-malware-deploys-xmrig-miner-on.html#link_share)\n_\uf1e0_ Share\n[_\ue80a_](javascript:void\\(0\\)) [_\uf09a_ Share on Facebook](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/go-based-malware-deploys-xmrig-miner-on.html#link_share) [_\uf099_ Share on Twitter](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/go-based-malware-deploys-xmrig-miner-on.html#link_share) [_\uf0e1_ Share on Linkedin](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/go-based-malware-deploys-xmrig-miner-on.html#link_share) [_\uf281_ Share on Reddit](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/go-based-malware-deploys-xmrig-miner-on.html#link_share) [_\uf1d4_ Share on Hacker News](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/go-based-malware-deploys-xmrig-miner-on.html#link_share) [_\uf0e0_ Share on Email](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/go-based-malware-deploys-xmrig-miner-on.html#link_share) [_\uf232_ Share on WhatsApp](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/go-based-malware-deploys-xmrig-miner-on.html#link_share) [![Facebook Messenger](h