ts\/images\/icon\/icon-categories.svg)\n![](https:\/\/securelist.com\/wp-content\/themes\/securelist2020\/assets\/images\/icon\/icon-categories--invert.svg)\nTable of Contents\n  * [Introduction](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#introduction)\n  * [nginx: the propagation malware](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#nginx-the-propagation-malware)\n    * [Infecting the container](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#infecting-the-container)\n    * [Spreading the infection](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#spreading-the-infection)\n      * [Host search](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#host-search)\n      * [Container creation](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#container-creation)\n      * [Compromising running containers](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#compromising-running-containers)\n  * [cloud \u2013 the Dero miner](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#cloud-the-dero-miner)\n  * [Takeaways](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#takeaways)\n  * [Indicators of compromise](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#indicators-of-compromise)\n\n\n![](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/21074153\/dero-miner-featured-image-upload-1-1200x600.jpg)\nAuthors\n  * [ ![](https:\/\/securelist.com\/wp-content\/themes\/securelist2020\/assets\/images\/avatar-default\/avatar_default_1.png) Amged Wageh](https:\/\/securelist.com\/author\/amgedwageh\/)\n\n\n## Introduction\nImagine a container zombie outbreak where a single infected container scans the internet for an exposed Docker API, and ~~bites~~ exploits it by creating new malicious containers and compromising the running ones, thus transforming them into new \u201czombies\u201d that will mine for Dero currency and continue \u201cbiting\u201d new victims. No command-and-control server is required for the delivery, just an exponentially growing number of victims that are automatically infecting new ones. That\u2019s exactly what the new Dero mining campaign does.\nDuring a recent [compromise assessment](https:\/\/www.kaspersky.com\/enterprise-security\/compromise-assessment?icid=gl_securelist_acq_ona_smm__onl_b2b_securelist_lnk_sm-team___kca____b9e6c9739af2d419) project, we detected a number of running containers with malicious activities. Some of the containers were previously recognized, while others were not. After forensically analyzing the containers, we confirmed that a threat actor was able to gain initial access to a running containerized infrastructure by exploiting an insecurely published Docker API. This led to the running containers being compromised and new ones being created not only to hijack the victim\u2019s resources for cryptocurrency mining but also to launch external attacks to propagate to other networks. The diagram below describes the attack vector:\n[![Infection chain](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20111744\/dero-miner-infects1.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20111744\/dero-miner-infects1.png)\nInfection chain\nThe entire attack vector is automated via two malware implants: the previously unknown propagation malware `nginx` and the Dero crypto miner. Both samples are written in Golang and packed with UPX. Kaspersky products detect these malicious implants with the following verdicts:\n  * nginx: `Trojan.Linux.Agent.gen`;\n  * Dero crypto miner: `RiskTool.Linux.Miner.gen`.\n\n\n## nginx: the propagation malware\nThis malware is responsible for maintaining the persistence of the crypto miner and its further propagation to external systems. This implant is designed to minimize interaction with the operator and does not require a delivery C2 server. `nginx` ensures that the malware spreads as long as there are users insecurely publishing their Docker APIs on the internet.\nThe malware is named \u201cnginx\u201d to masquerade as the well-known legitimate nginx web server software in an attempt to evade detection by users and security tools. In this post, we\u2019ll refer to this malware as \u201cnginx\u201d.\nAfter unpacking the `nginx` malware, we parsed the metadata of the Go binary and were able to determine the location of the Go source code file at compilation time: \u201c\/root\/shuju\/docker2375\/nginx.go\u201d.\n[![Nginx source code file](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20112151\/dero-miner-infects2.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20112151\/dero-miner-infects2.png)\nNginx source code file\n### Infecting the container\nThe malware starts by creating a log file at \u201c\/var\/log\/nginx.log\u201d.\n[![Log file creation](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20112243\/dero-miner-infects3.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20112243\/dero-miner-infects3.png)\nLog file creation\nThis log file will be used later to log the running activities of the malware, including data like the list of infected machines, the names of created malicious containers on those machines, and the exit status code if there were any errors.\n[![Malware operations log](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20112609\/dero-miner-infects4.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20112609\/dero-miner-infects4.png)\nMalware operations log\nAfter that, in a new process, a function called `main.checkVersion` loops infinitely to make sure that the content of a file located at \u201c\/usr\/bin\/version.dat\u201d inside the compromised container always equals `1.4`. If the file contents were changed, this function overwrites them.\n[![Ensuring that version.dat exists and contains 1.4](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20112652\/dero-miner-infects5.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20112652\/dero-miner-infects5.png)\nEnsuring that version.dat exists and contains 1.4\nIf `version.dat` doesn\u2019t exist, the malicious function creates this file with the content `1.4`, then sleeps for 24 hours before the next iteration.\n[![Creating version.dat if it doesn't exist](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20112757\/dero-miner-infects6.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20112757\/dero-miner-infects6.png)\nCreating version.dat if it doesn\u2019t exist\nThe malware uses the `version.dat` file to identify the already infected containers, which we\u2019ll describe later. The `nginx` sample then executes the `main.monitorCloudProcess` function that loops infinitely in a new process making sure that a process named `cloud`, which is a Dero miner, is running. First, the malware checks whether or not the `cloud` process is running. If it\u2019s not, `nginx` executes the `main.startCloudProcess` function to launch the miner.\n[![Monitoring and executing the cloud process](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20112909\/dero-miner-infects7.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20112909\/dero-miner-infects7.png)\nMonitoring and executing the cloud process\nIn order to execute the miner, the `main.startCloudProcess` function attempts to locate it at \u201c\/usr\/bin\/cloud\u201d.\n[![Executing the miner](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20112954\/dero-miner-infects8.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20112954\/dero-miner-infects8.png)\nExecuting the miner\n### Spreading the infection\n#### Host search\nNext, the `nginx` malware will go into an infinite loop of generating random IPv4 \/16 network subnets to scan them and compromise more networks with the `main.generateRandomSubnet` function.\n[![Infinite loop of network subnets generation and scanning](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20113059\/dero-miner-infects9.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20113059\/dero-miner-infects9.png)\nInfinite loop of network subnets generation and scanning\nThe subnets with the respective IP ranges will be passed to the `main.scanSubnet` function to be scanned via masscan, a port scanning tool installed in the container by the malware, which we will describe in more detail later. The scanner is looking for an insecure Docker API published on the internet to exploit by scanning the generated subnet via the following command: `masscan -p 2375 -oL \u2013 \u2013max-rate 360`.\n[![Scanning the generated subnet via masscan](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20113201\/dero-miner-infects10.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20113201\/dero-miner-infects10.png)\nScanning the generated subnet via masscan\nThe output of masscan is parsed via regex to extract the IPv4s that have the default Docker API port 2375 open. Then the extracted IPv4s are passed to the `main.checkDockerDaemon` function. It checks if the remote dockerd daemon on the host with a matching IPv4 is running and responsive. To do this, the malware attempts to list all running containers on the remote host by executing a `docker -H PS` command. If it fails, `nginx` proceeds to check the next IPv4.\n[![Remotely listing running containers](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20113248\/dero-miner-infects11.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20113248\/dero-miner-infects11.png)\nRemotely listing running containers\n#### Container creation\nAfter confirming that the remote dockerd daemon is running and responsive, `nginx` generates a container name with 12 random characters and uses it to create a malicious container on the remote target.\n[![Container name generation](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20113343\/dero-miner-infects12.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20113343\/dero-miner-infects12.png)\nContainer name generation\nThe malicious container is created with `docker -H run -dt \u2013name \u2013restart always ubuntu:18.04 \/bin\/bash`. The malware uses a `\u2013restart always` flag to start the newly created containers automatically when they exit.\n[![Malicious container created on a new host](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20113436\/dero-miner-infects13.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20113436\/dero-miner-infects13.png)\nMalicious container created on a new host\nThen `nginx` prepares the new container to install dependencies later by updating the packages via `docker -H exec apt-get -yq update`.\n[![Updating container packages](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20113518\/dero-miner-infects14.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20113518\/dero-miner-infects14.png)\nUpdating container packages\nNext, the malicious sample uses a `docker -H exec apt-get install -yq masscan docker.io` command to install masscan and docker.io in the container, which are dependencies for the malware to interact with the Docker daemon and to perform the external scan to infect other networks.\n[![Remotely installing the malware dependencies inside the newly created container](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20113555\/dero-miner-infects15.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20113555\/dero-miner-infects15.png)\nRemotely installing the malware dependencies inside the newly created container\nThen it transfers the two malicious implants, `nginx` and `cloud`, to the container by executing `docker -H cp -L \/usr\/bin\/ :\/usr\/bin`.\n[![Transferring nginx and cloud to the newly created container](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20113638\/dero-miner-infects16.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20113638\/dero-miner-infects16.png)\nTransferring nginx and cloud to the newly created container\nThe malware maintains persistence by adding the transferred `nginx` binary to `\/root\/.bash_aliases` to make sure that it will automatically execute upon shell login. This is done via a `docker -H exec bash \u2013norc -c \\'echo \\\"\/usr\/bin\/nginx &\\\" > \/root\/.bash_aliases\\'` command.\n[![Adding the nginx malware to .bash_aliases for persistence](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20113723\/dero-miner-infects17.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20113723\/dero-miner-infects17.png)\nAdding the nginx malware to .bash_aliases for persistence\n#### Compromising running containers\nUp until this point, the malware has only created new malicious containers. Now, it will try to compromise the ubuntu:18.04-based running containers. The sample first executes the `main.checkAndOperateContainers` function to check all the running containers on the remote vulnerable host for two conditions: the container has an ubuntu:18.04-base and it doesn\u2019t contain a `version.dat` file, which is an indicator that the container had been previously infected.\n[![Listing and compromising existing containers on the remote target](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20113943\/dero-miner-infects18.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20113943\/dero-miner-infects18.png)\nListing and compromising existing containers on the remote target\nIf these conditions are satisfied, the malware executes the `main.operateOnContainer` function to proceed with the [same attack vector](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#infecting-the-container) described earlier to infect the running container. The infection chain is repeated, hijacking the container resources to scan and compromise more containers and mining for the Dero cryptocurrency.\nThat way, the malware does not require a C2 connection and also maintains its activity as long as there is an insecurely published Docker API that can be exploited to compromise running containers and create new ones.\n## cloud \u2013 the Dero miner\nExecuting and maintaining `cloud`, the crypto miner, is the primary goal of the `nginx` sample. The miner is also written in Golang and packed with UPX. After unpacking the binary, we were able to attribute it to the open-source DeroHE CLI miner project found on GitHub. The threat actor wrapped the DeroHE CLI miner into the `cloud` malware, with a hardcoded mining configuration: a wallet address and a DeroHE node (derod) address.\nIf no addresses were passed as arguments, which is the case in this campaign, the `cloud` malware uses the hardcoded encrypted configuration as the default configuration. It is stored as a Base64-encoded string that, after decoding, results in an AES-CTR encrypted blob of a Base64-encoded wallet address, which is decrypted with the `main.decrypt` function. The configuration encryption indicates that the threat actors attempt to sophisticate the malware, as we haven\u2019t seen this in previous campaigns.\n[![Decrypting the crypto wallet address](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20114035\/dero-miner-infects19.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20114035\/dero-miner-infects19.png)\nDecrypting the crypto wallet address\nUpon decoding this string, we uncovered the wallet address in clear text: `dero1qyy8xjrdjcn2dvr6pwe40jrl3evv9vam6tpx537vux60xxkx6hs7zqgde993y`.\n[![Behavioral analysis of the decryption function](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20114120\/dero-miner-infects20.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20114120\/dero-miner-infects20.png)\nBehavioral analysis of the decryption function\nThen the malware decrypts another two hardcoded AES-CTR encrypted strings to get the dero node addresses via a function named `main.sockz`.\n[![Function calls to decrypt the addresses](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20114212\/dero-miner-infects21.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20114212\/dero-miner-infects21.png)\nFunction calls to decrypt the addresses\nThe node addresses are encrypted the same way the wallet address is, but with other keys. After decryption, we were able to obtain the following addresses: `d.windowsupdatesupport[.]link` and `h.wiNdowsupdatesupport[.]link`.\n[![Decoded addresses in memory](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20114301\/dero-miner-infects22.png)](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20114301\/dero-miner-infects22.png)\nDecoded addresses in memory\nThe same wallet address and the derod node addresses [had been observed before](https:\/\/www.crowdstrike.com\/en-us\/blog\/crowdstrike-discovers-first-ever-dero-cryptojacking-campaign-targeting-kubernetes\/) in a campaign that targeted Kubernetes clusters with Kubernetes API anonymous authentication enabled. Instead of transferring the malware to a compromised container, the threat actor pulls a malicious image named `pauseyyf\/pause:latest`, which is published on Docker Hub and contains the miner. This image was used to create the malicious container. Unlike the current campaign, the attack vector was meant to be stealthy as threat actors didn\u2019t attempt to move laterally or scan the internet to compromise more networks. These attacks [were seen throughout 2023 and 2024](https:\/\/www.wiz.io\/blog\/dero-cryptojacking-campaign-adapts-to-evade-detection) with minor changes in techniques.\n## Takeaways\nAlthough attacks on containers are less frequent than on other systems, they are not less dangerous. In the case we analyzed, containerized environments were compromised through a combination of a previously known miner and a new sample that created malicious containers and infected existing ones. The two malicious implants spread without a C2 server, making any network that has a containerized infrastructure and insecurely published Docker API to the internet a potential target.\nAnalysis of Shodan shows that in April 2025, there were 520 published Docker APIs over port 2375 worldwide. It highlights the potential destructive consequences of the described threat and emphasizes the need for thorough monitoring and container protection.\n_Docker APIs published over port 2375 ports worldwide, January\u2013April 2025 ([download](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/20114441\/dero-miner-infects23.png))_\nBuilding your containerized infrastructure from known legitimate images alone doesn\u2019t guarantee security. Just like any other system, containerized applications can be compromised at runtime, so it\u2019s crucial to monitor your containerized infrastructure with efficient monitoring tools like [Kaspersky Container Security](https:\/\/www.kaspersky.com\/kcs?icid=gl_securelist_acq_ona_smm__onl_b2b_securelist_lnk_sm-team___kcs____0483578632bd0fab). It detects misconfigurations and monitors registry images, ensuring the safety of container environments. We also recommend proactively hunting for threats to detect stealthy malicious activities and incidents that might have slipped unnoticed on your network. The [Kaspersky Compromise Assessment](https:\/\/www.kaspersky.com\/enterprise-security\/compromise-assessment?icid=gl_securelist_acq_ona_smm__onl_b2b_securelist_lnk_sm-team___kca____b9e6c9739af2d419) service can help you not only detect such incidents, but also remediate them and provide immediate and effective incident response activities.\n## Indicators of compromise\n**File hashes** [094085675570A18A9225399438471CC9](https:\/\/opentip.kaspersky.com\/094085675570a18a9225399438471cc9\/results?icid=gl_securelist_acq_ona_smm__onl_b2b_securelist_lnk_sm-team_______95e2142053ec647b&utm_source=SL&utm_medium=SL&utm_campaign=SL) nginx [14E7FB298049A57222254EF0F47464A7](https:\/\/opentip.kaspersky.com\/14e7fb298049a57222254ef0f47464a7\/results?icid=gl_securelist_acq_ona_smm__onl_b2b_securelist_lnk_sm-team_______bbd6fe1ecd8ab25c&utm_source=SL&utm_medium=SL&utm_campaign=SL) cloud\n**File paths** **NOTE: Certain file path IoCs may lead to false positives[due to the masquerading technique used](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#masquerading).** \/usr\/bin\/nginx \/usr\/bin\/cloud \/var\/log\/nginx.log \/usr\/bin\/version.dat\n**Derod nodes addresses** [d.windowsupdatesupport[.]link](https:\/\/opentip.kaspersky.com\/d.windowsupdatesupport.link\/?icid=gl_securelist_acq_ona_smm__onl_b2b_securelist_lnk_sm-team_______2ec825071b835fe6&utm_source=SL&utm_medium=SL&utm_campaign=SL) [h.wiNdowsupdatesupport[.]link](https:\/\/opentip.kaspersky.com\/h.windowsupdatesupport.link\/?icid=gl_securelist_acq_ona_smm__onl_b2b_securelist_lnk_sm-team_______8236f11dc70362f6&utm_source=SL&utm_medium=SL&utm_campaign=SL)\n**Dero wallet address** dero1qyy8xjrdjcn2dvr6pwe40jrl3evv9vam6tpx537vux60xxkx6hs7zqgde993y\n  * [Malware Technologies](https:\/\/securelist.com\/tag\/malware-technologies\/)\n  * [Linux](https:\/\/securelist.com\/tag\/linux\/)\n  * [Malware Descriptions](https:\/\/securelist.com\/tag\/malware-descriptions\/)\n  * [Malware](https:\/\/securelist.com\/tag\/malware\/)\n  * [Cryptocurrencies](https:\/\/securelist.com\/tag\/cryptocurrencies\/)\n  * [Miner](https:\/\/securelist.com\/tag\/miner\/)\n  * [Compromise assessment](https:\/\/securelist.com\/tag\/compromise-assessment\/)\n  * [Containers](https:\/\/securelist.com\/tag\/containers\/)\n  * [Docker](https:\/\/securelist.com\/tag\/docker\/)\n\n\nAuthors\n  * [ ![](https:\/\/securelist.com\/wp-content\/themes\/securelist2020\/assets\/images\/avatar-default\/avatar_default_1.png) Amged Wageh](https:\/\/securelist.com\/author\/amgedwageh\/)\n\n\nDero miner zombies biting through Docker APIs to build a cryptojacking horde\n![](https:\/\/securelist.com\/wp-content\/themes\/securelist2020\/assets\/images\/icon\/icon-categories.svg)\n![](https:\/\/securelist.com\/wp-content\/themes\/securelist2020\/assets\/images\/icon\/icon-categories--invert.svg)\nTable of Contents\n  * [Introduction](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#introduction)\n  * [nginx: the propagation malware](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#nginx-the-propagation-malware)\n    * [Infecting the container](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#infecting-the-container)\n    * [Spreading the infection](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#spreading-the-infection)\n      * [Host search](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#host-search)\n      * [Container creation](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#container-creation)\n      * [Compromising running containers](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#compromising-running-containers)\n  * [cloud \u2013 the Dero miner](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#cloud-the-dero-miner)\n  * [Takeaways](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#takeaways)\n  * [Indicators of compromise](https:\/\/securelist.com\/dero-miner-infects-containers-through-docker-api\/116546\/#indicators-of-compromise)\n\n\n##### Subscribe to our weekly e-mails\nThe hottest research right in your inbox\nIn the same category\n[ ![](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/04\/28151908\/SL-outlaw-featured-800x450.jpg) ](https:\/\/securelist.com\/outlaw-botnet\/116444\/)\n[ ![](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/04\/21075636\/SL-fake-CAPTCHA-featured-800x450.jpg) ](https:\/\/securelist.com\/lumma-fake-captcha-attacks-analysis\/116274\/)\n[ ![](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/02\/14115336\/webinar_Detection_Engineering_243x136-800x450.jpg) ](https:\/\/securelist.com\/streamlining-detection-engineering\/116186\/)\n[ ![](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/03\/11075637\/incident-response-report-2024-featured-image-800x450.jpg) ](https:\/\/securelist.com\/kaspersky-incident-response-report-2024\/115873\/)\n[ ![](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/02\/27084120\/SL-web-shell-story-featured-800x450.jpg) ](https:\/\/securelist.com\/soc-files-web-shell-chase\/115714\/)\n* [![](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2024\/04\/10092503\/NEXT_310x420_EN_1.jpg)](https:\/\/www.kaspersky.com\/next?icid=gl_KNext_acq_ona_smm__onl_b2b_securelist_ban_sm-team___knext___)\n##### Latest Posts\n[ ![](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/05\/06151046\/ransomware-report-2025-featured-image-800x450.jpg) ](https:\/\/securelist.com\/state-of-ransomware-in-2025\/116475\/)\n[ ![](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/04\/28151908\/SL-outlaw-featured-800x450.jpg) ](https:\/\/securelist.com\/outlaw-botnet\/116444\/)\n[ ![](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/04\/25081114\/triada-report-featured-image-800x450.jpg) ](https:\/\/securelist.com\/triada-trojan-modules-analysis\/116380\/)\n[ ![](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/04\/23140753\/operation-synchole-featured-image-800x450.jpg) ](https:\/\/securelist.com\/operation-synchole-watering-hole-attacks-by-lazarus\/116326\/)\n##### Latest Webinars\n[ ![](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/03\/19103030\/Report_IR_cover_wide-800x450.jpg) ](https:\/\/securelist.com\/webinars\/in-depth-analysis-of-cyberattacks-key-findings-from-kasperskys-incident-response-report\/)\n[ ![](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/02\/20111534\/SL-MDR-featured-800x450.jpg) ](https:\/\/securelist.com\/webinars\/silent-shields-digital-dragons-mdrs-proactive-protection\/)\n[ ![](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2025\/02\/14115336\/webinar_Detection_Engineering_243x136-800x450.jpg) ](https:\/\/securelist.com\/webinars\/from-chaos-to-control-streamlining-detection-engineering-in-security-operation-centers\/)\n[ ![](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2024\/12\/20171256\/webinar_crimeware_ksb-800x450.jpg) ](https:\/\/securelist.com\/webinars\/%d1%81rimeware-and-financial-cyberthreats-in-2025\/)\n##### Reports\nKaspersky GReAT experts uncovered a new campaign by Lazarus APT that exploits vulnerabilities in South Korean software products and uses a watering hole approach.\nMysterySnail RAT attributed to IronHusky APT group hasn\u2019t been reported since 2021. Recently, Kaspersky GReAT detected new versions of this implant in government organizations in Mongolia and Russia.\nKaspersky researchers analyze GOFFEE\u2019s campaign in H2 2024: the updated infection scheme, new PowerModul implant, switch to a binary Mythic agent.\nKaspersky GReAT experts discovered a complex APT attack on Russian organizations dubbed Operation ForumTroll, which exploits zero-day vulnerabilities in Google Chrome.\n[![](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2020\/12\/12163122\/scan_engine_webinar_370x500.jpg)](https:\/\/www.brighttalk.com\/webcast\/15591\/641371?icid=gl_securelist_acq_ona_smm__onl_b2b_securelist_ban_sm-team___kse____986fd00cfd34786a)\n##### Subscribe to our weekly e-mails\nThe hottest research right in your inbox\n[![](https:\/\/media.kasperskycontenthub.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/sites\/43\/2020\/12\/12163132\/Scan_Engine_webinar_800x800-740x740.jpg)](https:\/\/www.brighttalk.com\/webcast\/15591\/641371?icid=gl_securelist_acq_ona_smm__onl_b2b_securelist_ban_sm-team___kse____986fd00cfd34786a)\n[Threats](https:\/\/securelist.com\/threat-categories\/)\nThreats\n  * [APT (Targeted attacks)](https:\/\/securelist.com\/threat-category\/apt-targeted-attacks\/)\n  * [Secure environment (IoT)](https:\/\/securelist.com\/threat-category\/secure-environment\/)\n  * [Mobile threats](https:\/\/securelist.com\/threat-cate