"markdown": "[_\ue80a_](javascript:void\\(0\\))\nContact\/Tip Us\n[ _\uf0e0_ Reach out to get featured\u2014contact us to send your exclusive story idea, research, hacks, or ask us a question or leave a comment\/feedback! ](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/p\/submit-news.html)\nFollow Us On Social Media\n[_\uf167_](https:\/\/www.youtube.com\/c\/thehackernews?sub_confirmation=1)\n[_\uf09e_ RSS Feeds](https:\/\/feeds.feedburner.com\/TheHackersNews) [_\uf0f3_ Email Alerts](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html#email-outer) [_\uf2c6_ Telegram Channel](https:\/\/t.me\/+wKrQxniFO1Q1OTRk)\n[![Zenith Live 2025](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEiKLqdTf2BnL4fIex_Fj-HhjsQU6HOZ_ao_WyUZ0ELrxsZ4ezufQqD8JfJabnIMeCtgdwKU-y948oMzHGFOP_Ox3vMW1Ymki-zmY4bSHWFP-m4zwD42-Huw3q9eM8VAY9_mL9yzihdsF1YygoUH-dDrY8EzKJb4dslf03xMaGFlYgAy0bA6p7gZ5vr5GHHb\/s728-e300\/team-d.jpg)](https:\/\/thehackernews.uk\/team-d)\n# [PureRAT Malware Spikes 4x in 2025, Deploying PureLogs to Target Russian Firms](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html)\n_\ue802_ May 21, 2025 _\ue804_ Ravie LakshmananMalware \/ Windows Security\n[![](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html)](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEhlT861CEuDL251pD5JBHg7_WFJ6_xoUwg7Vmy8duI3yyJq7YCVQEB7thiCELPXODcD6In8VAmsCJk131JUZZfWvTTHDDZKIYna1OH_a0gGAihayAqHdtx5bknIMqceJ9T-HaYfGKjdkx6n_Kau5nS_YKEV4MUl4UbjejEQhN0MdD51_RAT4Gl-DL2W03Za\/s728-rw-e365\/dll.jpg)\nRussian organizations have become the target of a phishing campaign that distributes malware called PureRAT, according to new findings from Kaspersky.\n\"The campaign aimed at Russian business began back in March 2023, but in the first third of 2025 the number of attacks quadrupled compared to the same period in 2024,\" the cybersecurity vendor [said](https:\/\/securelist.ru\/purerat-attacks-russian-organizations\/112619\/).\nThe attack chains, which have not been attributed to any specific threat actor, commence with a phishing email that contains a RAR file attachment or a link to the archive that masquerades as a Microsoft Word or a PDF document by making use of double extensions (\"doc_054_[redacted].pdf.rar\").\nPresent within the archive file is an executable that, when launched, copies itself to the \"%AppData%\" location of the compromised Windows machine under the name \"task.exe\" and creates a Visual Basic Script called \"Task.vbs\" in the Startup VBS folder.\n[![Cybersecurity](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html)](https:\/\/thehackernews.uk\/drata-d-2 \"Cybersecurity\")\nThe executable then proceeds to unpack another executable \"ckcfb.exe\", runs the system utility \"InstallUtil.exe,\" and injects into it the decrypted module. \"Ckcfb.exe,\" for its part, extracts and decrypts a DLL file \"Spydgozoi.dll\" that incorporates the main payload of the PureRAT malware.\nPureRAT establishes SSL connections with a command-and-control (C2) server and transmits system information, including details about the antivirus products installed, the computer name, and the time elapsed since the system startup. In response, the C2 server sends auxiliary modules to perform a variety of malicious actions -\n  * PluginPcOption, which is capable of executing commands for self-deletion, restarting the executable file, and shutting down or rebooting the computer\n  * PluginWindowNotify, which checks the name of the active window for keywords like password, bank, WhatsApp, and perform appropriate follow-up actions like unauthorized fund transfers\n  * PluginClipper, which functions as a [clipper malware](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/04\/cryptocurrency-miner-and-clipper.html) by substituting cryptocurrency wallet addresses copied to the system's clipboard with an attacker-controlled one\n\n\n\"The Trojan includes modules for downloading and running arbitrary files that provide full access to the file system, registry, processes, camera and microphone, implement keylogger functionality, and give attackers the ability to secretly control the computer using the remote desktop principle,\" Kaspersky said.\n[![](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html)](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEigJNJ63ZYTVGwrswg8dC3vvnnszSBfjfg4Cv1HUGqbh6nrsg5qWk505sRzdC6ksUiJ5lEEYBvw1vSMsKRBgh0KJmJAjkMwrxsGXnzCPCoi3aEnITibIiCiaEiEpIEu2JEItsIjkDYInz1bZlKMl2mZTuGVWM03rNsT4TNOE09AkYBTdPIkYMqPWh3M3Yv6\/s728-rw-e365\/exe.jpg)\nThe original executable that launches \"ckcfb.exe\" simultaneously also extracts a second binary referred to as \"StilKrip.exe,\" which is a commercially available downloader dubbed [PureCrypter](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2023\/02\/purecrypter-malware-targets-government.html) that has been used to deliver various payloads in the past. It's active since 2022. \n[![Cybersecurity](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html)](https:\/\/thehackernews.uk\/tines-incident-response-d \"Cybersecurity\")\n\"StilKrip.exe\" is designed to download \"Bghwwhmlr.wav,\" which follows the aforementioned attack sequence to run \"InstallUtil.exe\" and ultimately launch \"Ttcxxewxtly.exe,\" an executable that unpacks and runs a DLL payload called PureLogs (\"Bftvbho.dll\").\nPureLogs is an [off-the-shelf information stealer](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2024\/08\/cybercriminals-abusing-cloudflare.html) that can harvest data from web browsers, email clients, VPN services, messaging apps, wallet browser extensions, password managers, cryptocurrency wallet apps, and other programs like FileZilla and WinSCP.\n\"The PureRAT backdoor and PureLogs stealer have broad functionality that allows attackers to gain unlimited access to infected systems and confidential organization data,\" Kaspersky said. \"The main vector of attacks on businesses has been and remains emails with malicious attachments or links.\"\nFound this article interesting? Follow us on and to read more exclusive content we post.\nSHARE [_\uf09a_](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html#link_share) [_\uf099_](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html#link_share) [_\uf0e1_](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html#link_share) [_\uf1e0_](javascript:void\\(0\\))\n[_\uf099_ Tweet](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html#link_share)\n[_\uf0e1_ Share](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html#link_share)\n[_\uf09a_ Share](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html#link_share)\n_\uf1e0_ Share\n[_\ue80a_](javascript:void\\(0\\)) [_\uf09a_ Share on Facebook](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html#link_share) [_\uf099_ Share on Twitter](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html#link_share) [_\uf0e1_ Share on Linkedin](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html#link_share) [_\uf281_ Share on Reddit](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html#link_share) [_\uf1d4_ Share on Hacker News](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html#link_share) [_\uf0e0_ Share on Email](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html#link_share) [_\uf232_ Share on WhatsApp](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html#link_share) [![Facebook Messenger](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html)Share on Facebook Messenger](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malware-spikes-4x-in-2025.html#link_share) [_\uf2c6_ Share on Telegram](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/purerat-malw