"markdown": "![Google](https:\/\/cms.therecord.media\/uploads\/format_webp\/large_Google_2_c3f51c477a.jpg)\nCredit: Karollyne Videira Hubert \/ Unsplash\n[Suzanne Smalley](https:\/\/therecord.media\/author\/suzanne-smalley)May 21st, 2025\n# New Google program targeting children with AI chatbot may violate FTC privacy rules\nGoogle\u2019s recent launch of a program targeting its AI-powered Gemini chatbot at children under age 13 has prompted outrage from privacy and children\u2019s rights advocates who on Wednesday told the Federal Trade Commission (FTC) the effort appears to violate the agency\u2019s privacy rule protecting children online. \nThe tech giant recently sent emails to parents signed up for [Family Link](https:\/\/families.google\/familylink\/) \u2014 which allows them to create Gmail accounts for their children and offers other special services \u2014 advising them that their children can engage with the chatbot on homework or in conversation to \u201ccreate stories, songs, and poetry.\u201d \nChildren with parent-controlled Google accounts will be able to independently access the chatbot, according to an email to parents seen by Recorded Future News. The email said that Google will notify parents if their children do access the chatbot, allowing them to disable access at that point. \nIn a scathing [letter](https:\/\/www.documentcloud.org\/documents\/25950860-letter-to-ftc-re-google-gemini-epic-and-fairplay-52125\/) to the Federal Trade Commission, the Electronic Privacy Information Center (EPIC) and the children\u2019s advocacy group Fairplay condemned Google for alerting parents to the change \u201cvia an email that indicates the company will not be requiring verifiable parental consent before engaging in this new form of data collection and use.\u201d \nIn addition to indicating that parents will need to opt out of the program, not in, Google acknowledged in the missive that parents should be aware that children \u201cmay encounter content you don\u2019t want them to see\u201d and that \u201cGemini can make mistakes.\u201d \n\u201cLet your children know not to enter sensitive or personal info in Gemini,\u201d the email says, cautioning parents to encourage children to \u201cthink critically\u201d about interactions with the chatbot. \nThe New York Times [first reported](https:\/\/www.nytimes.com\/2025\/05\/02\/technology\/google-gemini-ai-chatbot-kids.html) the new program and the contents of the email. \nAn online Google [explainer](https:\/\/support.google.com\/gemini\/answer\/16109150?hl=en) of the program details how exactly parents can opt out of having their children use Gemini. \n\u201cIf you turn off Gemini Apps, your child won\u2019t be able to sign in to the app on any Android device or Gemini on the web,\u201d the explainer says. \nGoogle declined to comment. \nNew FTC Chair Andrew Ferguson has expressed special concern about protecting children\u2019s online privacy and recently allowed an updated version of the Children\u2019s Online Privacy Protection Act (COPPA), which was finalized under his predecessor Lina Khan, to [go into effect](https:\/\/therecord.media\/ftc-coppa-childrens-data-privacy-updated-regulation) on or around June 23. \nFerguson on Thursday [told Congress](https:\/\/therecord.media\/ftc-ferguson-wont-regulate-ai-until-problems-arise) he is aware that lawmakers have been \u201cespecially solicitous\u201d about children\u2019s privacy online. He made the comments in written testimony submitted to the House Appropriations Committee\u2019s Subcommittee on Financial Services and General Government. \nNoting that the collection, aggregation, disclosure and retention of children\u2019s personal data \u201cposes real dangers\u201d to them, Ferguson said the COPPA Rule applies to website operators and online services with \u201cactual knowledge\u201d of such collection from children under age 13. \nHe also noted that the COPPA rule mandates that providers obtain \u201cverifiable parental consent\u201d before gathering, using or sharing data belonging to children in that age range. \n\u201cSome of the Commission\u2019s most valuable work has been the enforcement of COPPA and the COPPA Rule,\u201d Ferguson wrote in his testimony. \u201cFor that same reason, protecting children and teens online is similarly of paramount importance\u201d to the new administration. \nIn addition to asking the FTC to investigate Google\u2019s new program, EPIC and Fairplay wrote a [letter](https:\/\/www.documentcloud.org\/documents\/25950859-google-gemini-letter-5212025\/) to Google CEO Sundar Pichai asking the company to put its rollout of Gemini to children under age 13 on hold, arguing that generative AI and chatbots can harm children\u2019s mental health and social development. \nGoogle has said children\u2019s data will not be used to train AI, but the advocacy organizations told Pichai that Google also has \u201cnot identified additional safeguards to ensure that it would not misuse data collected through Gemini for other internal purposes or share it with third parties.\u201d \n  * [](https:\/\/news.ycombinator.com\/submitlink?u=&t=New Google program targeting children with AI chatbot may violate FTC privacy rules)\n  * [](https:\/\/bsky.app\/intent\/compose?text=New Google program targeting children with AI chatbot may violate FTC privacy rules )\n\n\n  * [News](https:\/\/therecord.media\/)\n  * [Privacy](https:\/\/therecord.media\/news\/privacy)\n\n\nGet more insights with the \nRecorded Future\nIntelligence Cloud.\n[Learn more.](https:\/\/www.recordedfuture.com\/platform?mtm_campaign=ad-unit-record)\nTags\n  * [Google](https:\/\/therecord.media\/tag\/google)\n  * [artificial intelligence (AI)](https:\/\/therecord.media\/tag\/artificial-intelligence)\n  * [Children\u2019s Online Privacy Protection Rule (COPPA)](https:\/\/therecord.media\/tag-childrens-online-privacy-protection-rule-coppa)\n  * [Privacy](https:\/\/therecord.media\/tag\/privacy)\n\n\nNo previous article\nNo new articles\n[![Suzanne Smalley](https:\/\/cms.therecord.media\/uploads\/format_webp\/Suzanne_Smalley_27e80bc465.png)](https:\/\/therecord.media\/author\/suzanne-smalley)\n[Suzanne Smalley](https:\/\/therecord.media\/author\/suzanne-smalley)\nis a reporter covering privacy, disinformation and cybersecurity policy for The Record. She was previously a cybersecurity reporter at CyberScoop and Reuters. Earlier in her career Suzanne covered the Boston Police Department for the Boston Globe and two presidential campaign cycles for Newsweek. She lives in Washington with her husband and three children.\n## Briefs\n  * [Midwestern telco Cellcom confirms cyber incident after days of service outagesMay 21st, 2025](https:\/\/therecord.media\/midwestern-cellcom-telcom-cyberattack)\n  * [Senators question Noem about CISA cuts, but get few detailsMay 20th, 2025](https:\/\/therecord.media\/noem-cisa-cuts-senate-hearing)\n  * [Ohio\u2019s Kettering Health system facing widespread outages after cyberattackMay 20th, 2025](https:\/\/therecord.media\/kettering-health-system-ohio-cyberattack)\n  * [Cyberthreat to Alabama state government \u2018neutralized\u2019May 20th, 2025](https:\/\/therecord.media\/alabama-cyberattack-neutralized)\n  * [Major Russian state services disrupted, reportedly due to cyberattackMay 20th, 2025](https:\/\/therecord.media\/major-russian-state-services-disrupted-ddos)\n  * [Dutch government passes law to criminalize cyber-espionageMay 20th, 2025](https:\/\/therecord.media\/netherlands-law-criminalizes-cyber-espionage)\n  * [Hack of SEC social media account earns 14-month prison sentence for Alabama manMay 16th, 2025](https:\/\/therecord.media\/sec-social-media-hack-sentencing)\n  * [OPM urged to continue identity protection contracts shielding people affected by 2015 hackMay 16th, 2025](https:\/\/therecord.media\/opm-federal-employee-data-protection-contracts-2015-hack-warner)\n  * [EU court rules that tracking-based online ads are illegalMay 16th, 2025](https:\/\/therecord.media\/eu-court-rules-tracking-based-ads-illegal)\n\n\n## [Measuring the US-China AI Gap![Measuring the US-China AI Gap](https:\/\/cms.recordedfuture.com\/uploads\/format_webp\/BLOG_ta_2025_0508_Main_Feature_0873ea3526.jpg)](https:\/\/www.recordedfuture.com\/research\/measuring-the-us-china-ai-gap)\n## [TerraStealerV2 and TerraLogger: Golden Chickens' New Malware Families Discovered![TerraStealerV2 and TerraLogger: Golden Chickens' New Malware Families Discovered](https:\/\/cms.recordedfuture.com\/uploads\/format_webp\/cta_2025_0501_Blog_Main_Feature_55a0e64897.jpg)](https:\/\/www.recordedfuture.com\/research\/terrastealerv2-and-terralogger)\n## [Uncovering MintsLoader With Recorded Future Malware Intelligence Hunting![Uncovering MintsLoader With Recorded Future Malware Intelligence Hunting](https:\/\/cms.recordedfuture.com\/uploads\/format_webp\/BLOG_cta_2025_0429_Main_Feature_e924c36cbd.jpg)](https:\/\/www.recordedfuture.com\/research\/uncovering-mintsloader-with-recorded-future-malware-intelligence-hunting)\n## [Iran\u2019s AI Ambitions: Balancing Economic Isolation with National Security Imperatives![Iran\u2019s AI Ambitions: Balancing Economic Isolation with National Security Imperatives](https:\/\/cms.recordedfuture.com\/uploads\/format_webp\/irans_ai_ambitions_balancing_economic_isolation_national_security_imperatives_151fba814d.png)](https:\/\/www.recordedfuture.com\/research\/irans-ai-ambitions-balancing-economic-isolation-national-security-imperatives)\n## [2024 Malicious Infrastructure Report![2024 Malicious Infrastructure Report](https:\/\/cms.recordedfuture.com\/uploads\/format_webp\/2024_malicious_infrastructure_report_95f6761bdf.png)](https:\/\/www.recordedfuture.com\/research\/2024-malicious-infrastructure-report)\n",,
