"title": "Critical Windows Server 2025 dMSA Vulnerability Enables Active Directory Compromise",,
"url": "https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/critical-windows-server-2025-dmsa.html",,
"summary": "A privilege escalation flaw in Windows Server 2025 enables attackers to compromise any Active Directory (AD) user by exploiting the Delegated Managed Service Account (dMSA) feature. The flaw was discovered by Akamai security researcher Yuval Gordon, and the attack technique has been codenamed 'BadSuccessor.' The vulnerability affects organizations using Active Directory in default configurations and leverages dMSA's ability to supersede existing service accounts. During dMSA migration, permissions are transferred, potentially allowing privilege escalation. Though Akamai reported the issue as of moderate severity and Microsoft is working on a patch, no immediate fix is available. Akamai recommends limiting dMSA creation and hardening permissions.",,
