"markdown": "[_\ue80a_](javascript:void\\(0\\))\nContact\/Tip Us\n[ _\uf0e0_ Reach out to get featured\u2014contact us to send your exclusive story idea, research, hacks, or ask us a question or leave a comment\/feedback! ](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/p\/submit-news.html)\nFollow Us On Social Media\n[_\uf167_](https:\/\/www.youtube.com\/c\/thehackernews?sub_confirmation=1)\n[_\uf09e_ RSS Feeds](https:\/\/feeds.feedburner.com\/TheHackersNews) [_\uf0f3_ Email Alerts](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/hackers-use-fake-vpn-and-browser-nsis.html#email-outer) [_\uf2c6_ Telegram Channel](https:\/\/t.me\/+wKrQxniFO1Q1OTRk)\n[![Zenith Live 2025](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEj_qMszEYVjwFa9p_Krb5PlKHZJ_qBguBZqxrUfXlqHzxZp2oUt8yjE6vjlrLVTK0Y1GtUlNL0C05SY-iq5GFsNkYrpPSTbPj30XarU3aO5dCJ-JkolvzdnxuynVOr9iXHx-dxJxuf-Ie7jyStTAnUzs8echAPUUsWcrutKu-pumRQLp2UIDfddyBqzblfx\/s728-e300\/training-d.jpg)](https:\/\/thehackernews.uk\/training-d)\n# [Hackers Use Fake VPN and Browser NSIS Installers to Deliver Winos 4.0 Malware](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/hackers-use-fake-vpn-and-browser-nsis.html)\n_\ue802_ May 25, 2025 _\ue804_ Ravie LakshmananThreat Intelligence \/ Software Security\n[![Winos 4.0 Malware](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEhlPB2vl_nzxvdQf-t0Lkjof6hugcJ6SJ2dTkMrzNVkGz5D-6SHCaTvguA7tlJHC-IPClptVpMlDAMhFrLsvZhj2_4SeQWrUD-5LYGx-UL0aFYdnMaR30ZE-3mIC5fPBn8DVYkppmGuP4cRzRnZ25xMYDrN0EHFl_7cIHW9FTmYSE89aWGaAjBrjbES_QZZ\/s728-rw-e365\/malware-install.jpg)](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEhlPB2vl_nzxvdQf-t0Lkjof6hugcJ6SJ2dTkMrzNVkGz5D-6SHCaTvguA7tlJHC-IPClptVpMlDAMhFrLsvZhj2_4SeQWrUD-5LYGx-UL0aFYdnMaR30ZE-3mIC5fPBn8DVYkppmGuP4cRzRnZ25xMYDrN0EHFl_7cIHW9FTmYSE89aWGaAjBrjbES_QZZ\/s728-rw-e365\/malware-install.jpg)\nCybersecurity researchers have disclosed a malware campaign that uses fake software installers masquerading as popular tools like LetsVPN and QQ Browser to deliver the **Winos 4.0** framework.\nThe campaign, first detected by Rapid7 in February 2025, involves the use of a multi-stage, memory-resident loader called Catena.\n\"Catena uses embedded shellcode and configuration switching logic to stage payloads like Winos 4.0 entirely in memory, evading traditional antivirus tools,\" security researchers Anna \u0160irokova and Ivan Feigl [said](https:\/\/www.rapid7.com\/blog\/post\/2025\/05\/22\/nsis-abuse-and-srdi-shellcode-anatomy-of-the-winos-4-0-campaign\/). \"Once installed, it quietly connects to attacker-controlled servers \u2013 mostly hosted in Hong Kong \u2013 to receive follow-up instructions or additional malware.\"\nThe attacks, like those that have deployed Winos 4.0 in the past, appear to focus specifically on Chinese-speaking environments, with the cybersecurity company calling out the \"careful, long-term planning\" by a very capable threat actor.\nWinos 4.0 (aka ValleyRAT) was [first publicly documented](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2024\/06\/void-arachne-uses-deepfakes-and-ai-to.html) by Trend Micro in June 2024 as used in attacks targeting Chinese-speaking users by means of malicious Windows Installer (MSI) files for VPN apps. The activity has been attributed to a threat cluster it tracks as Void Arachne, which is also referred to as Silver Fox.\n[![Cybersecurity](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/hackers-use-fake-vpn-and-browser-nsis.html)](https:\/\/thehackernews.uk\/drata-d-1 \"Cybersecurity\")\nSubsequent campaigns distributing the malware have [leveraged](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2024\/11\/new-winos-40-malware-infects-gamers.html) gaming-related applications like installation tools, speed boosters, and optimization utilities as lures to trick users into installing it. Another attack wave [detailed](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/02\/silver-fox-apt-uses-winos-40-malware-in.html) in February 2025 targeted entities in Taiwan via phishing emails that purported to be from the National Taxation Bureau.\nBuilt atop the foundations of a known remote access trojan called Gh0st RAT, Winos 4.0 is an advanced malicious framework written in C++ that makes use of a plugin-based system to harvest data, provide remote shell access, and launch distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attacks.\n[![Winos 4.0 Malware](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/hackers-use-fake-vpn-and-browser-nsis.html)](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEinfQTUxD_MFApUudbe7O7uonYH7_dSp3Z3U-ewrQs7UnGfLAsE3WlNzkVrcrtjI8bYMdIPSncCR1o0akbF2jCYZENIyiAC6S5hXDa5Em4EI22VF5rXDwQ6PkHBVhZnq5o71GrJczSQ8WGLQY9o4r3lHScVIjztVkkZd92ojRw42rJue8F_a5ErnKVR0DX5\/s728-rw-e365\/1.jpg)  \n---  \nQQBrowser-Based Infection Flow Observed in February 2025  \nRapid7 said all the artifacts flagged in February 2025 relied on NSIS installers bundled with signed decoy apps, shellcode embedded in \".ini\" files, and reflective DLL injection to covertly maintain persistence on infected hosts and avoid detection. The entire infection chain has been given the moniker Catena.\n\"The campaign has so far been active throughout 2025, showing a consistent infection chain with some tactical adjustments \u2013 pointing to a capable and adaptive threat actor,\" the researchers said.\nThe starting point is a trojanized [NSIS](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2024\/06\/hackers-exploit-legitimate-packer.html) installer impersonating an installer for QQ Browser, a Chromium-based web browser developed by Tencent, that's designed to deliver Winos 4.0 using Catena. The malware communicates with hard-coded command-and-control (C2) infrastructure over TCP port 18856 and HTTPS port 443.\n[![Winos 4.0 Malware](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/hackers-use-fake-vpn-and-browser-nsis.html)](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEiRdModoOxpSz6qoEHjwuXEQHmXZtaKMQEOIGvnBCJkiDLRSg6ufT1pRQFS-4NfsYk6cY_Ywdd6tztrNxQVmLKsMEwchOOI6lqNqdqTIRgAtNiQD0VsIWOWBITy4TvFhoAWCzCyfAljxFOoV3-e-SLOho3D65rEKM-wKuI7jIkcBm8CQdPox16WLm51ZfsN\/s728-rw-e365\/eee.jpg)  \n---  \nFrom LetsVPN Installer to Winos 4.0 in April 2025  \nPersistence on the host is achieved by registering scheduled tasks that are executed weeks after the initial compromise. While the malware features an explicit check to look for Chinese language settings on the system, it still proceeds with the execution even if that's not the case.\nThis indicates it's an unfinished feature and something that's expected to be implemented in subsequent iterations of the malware. That said, Rapid7 said it identified in April 2025 a \"tactical shift\" that not only switched some of the elements of the Catena execution chain, but also incorporated features to evade antivirus detection.\n[![Cybersecurity](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/hackers-use-fake-vpn-and-browser-nsis.html)](https:\/\/thehackernews.uk\/tines-incident-response-d \"Cybersecurity\")\nIn the revamped attack sequence, the NSIS installer disguises itself as a setup file for LetsVPN and runs a PowerShell command that adds [Microsoft Defender exclusions](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2024\/11\/cybercriminals-exploit-popular-game.html) for all drives (C:\\ to Z:\\\\). It then drops additional payloads, including an executable that takes a snapshot of running processes and checks for processes related to 360 Total Security, an antivirus product developed by Chinese vendor Qihoo 360.\nThe binary is signed with an expired certificate issued by VeriSign and allegedly belongs to Tencent Technology (Shenzhen). It was valid from 2018-10-11 to 2020-02-02. The primary responsibility of the executable is to reflectively load a DLL file that, in turn, connects to a C2 server (\"134.122.204[.]11:18852\" or \"103.46.185[.]44:443\") in order to download and execute Winos 4.0.\n\"This campaign shows a well-organized, regionally focused malware operation using trojanized NSIS installers to quietly drop the Winos 4.0 stager,\" the researchers said.\n\"It leans heavily on memory-resident payloads, reflective DLL loading, and decoy software signed with legit certificates to avoid raising alarms. Infrastructure overlaps and language-based targeting hint at ties to Silver Fox APT, with activity likely aimed at Chinese-speaking environments.\"\nFound this article interesting? Follow us on and to read more exclusive content we post.\nSHARE [_\uf1e0_](javascript:void\\(0\\))\n_\uf1e0_ Share\n[_\ue80a_](javascript:void\\(0\\)) [_\uf1d4_ Share on Hacker News](https:\/\/news.ycombinator.com\/submitlink?u=https%3A%2F%2Fthehackernews.com%2F2025%2F05%2Fhackers-use-fake-vpn-and-browser-nsis.html&t=Hackers%20Use%20Fake%20VPN%20and%20Browser%20NSIS%20Installers%20to%20Deliver%20Winos%204.0%20Malware) _\uf0e0_ Share on Email [_\uf232_ Share on WhatsApp](https:\/\/api.whatsapp.com\/send?text=Hackers%20Use%20Fake%20VPN%20and%20Browser%20NSIS%20Installers%20to%20Deliver%20Winos%204.0%20Malware \u2014 https%3A%2F%2Fthehackernews.com%2F2025%2F05%2Fhackers-use-fake-vpn-and-browser-nsis.html) [![Facebook Messenger](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/hackers-use-fake-vpn-and-browser-nsis.html)Share on Facebook Messenger](fb-messenger:\/\/share\/?link=https%3A%2F%2Fthehackernews.com%2F2025%2F05%2Fhackers-use-fake-vpn-and-browser-nsis.html&app_id=280117418781535) [_\uf2c6_ Share on Telegram](https:\/\/telegram.me\/share\/url?url=https%3A%2F%2Fthehackernews.com%2F2025%2F05%2Fhackers-use-fake-vpn-and-browser-nsis.html&text=Hackers%20Use%20Fake%20VPN%20and%20Browser%20NSIS%20Installers%20to%20Deliver%20Winos%204.0%20Malware)\n[SHARE _\uf1e0_](javascript:void\\(0\\))\n[Antivirus](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/search\/label\/Antivirus)[china](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/search\/label\/china)[cybersecurity](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/search\/label\/cybersecurity)[Digital Forensic](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/search\/label\/Digital%20Forensic)[Malware](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/search\/label\/Malware)[Phishing](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/search\/label\/Phish