"title": "Australian ransomware victims now must tell the government if they pay up",,
"url": "https:\/\/therecord.media\/australia-ransomware-victims-must-report-payments",,
"summary": "Australia has enacted a law requiring organizations with an annual turnover greater than AUS $3 million, and specific entities within critical infrastructure sectors, to report any ransomware payments to the government within 72 hours. The intention is to gain visibility over ransomware threats and their economic and social impact. The law affects approximately the top 6.5% of registered businesses, impacting about half of Australia's economy. Failure to report can result in penalties. The law aims to combat ransomware by increasing transparency, following high-profile attacks on companies like Optus and Medibank. While similar regulations have been proposed in other countries, such as the United Kingdom, experts suggest mandatory reporting may not directly reduce ransomware attacks.",,
