"markdown": "[_\ue80a_](javascript:void\\(0\\))\nContact\/Tip Us\n[ _\uf0e0_ Reach out to get featured\u2014contact us to send your exclusive story idea, research, hacks, or ask us a question or leave a comment\/feedback! ](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/p\/submit-news.html)\nFollow Us On Social Media\n[_\uf167_](https:\/\/www.youtube.com\/c\/thehackernews?sub_confirmation=1)\n[_\uf09e_ RSS Feeds](https:\/\/feeds.feedburner.com\/TheHackersNews) [_\uf0f3_ Email Alerts](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/researchers-uncover-20-configuration.html#email-outer) [_\uf2c6_ Telegram Channel](https:\/\/t.me\/+wKrQxniFO1Q1OTRk)\n[![Maximizing Efficiency and Security in Government Cloud Environments](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEjmy-PEKftk11FkFxOmdctMAQvuNZs31RGGDpxe66f8A72_iHu6NGjK0aDPU80YpR1m3Yo1zLbr-jR6RpBlz7xcjBXRasIVB2ELtOxV39r54fCWfLtySFA6x5eeDix9sZtk4aXcEBSQaEqR-y1EY_wtaQI34gSbWHYLpRqj9oUOE12EE2S3xGVTsoU9TIZ8\/s728-e100\/gov-cloud-efficiency-d.png)](https:\/\/thehackernews.uk\/gov-cloud-efficiency-d)\n# [Researchers Uncover 20+ Configuration Risks, Including Five CVEs, in Salesforce Industry Cloud](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/researchers-uncover-20-configuration.html)\n_\ue802_ Jun 10, 2025 _\ue804_ Ravie LakshmananVulnerability \/ SaaS Security\n[![Salesforce Industry Cloud](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEjOD6ZYqSFsGpRVJxBtltcRoC9-bFtsZJmqFifYX8Igo5yrPbPOmILwcgX3aZrxE52OMY7IzAwCXNy5K_Xd485g9g3SA2KnS0CoIAwsnUkRpDsF4vFlna2kiWl3wy1h2OWm_oFqyi0EIW3qSGaz8K8s0dXRqc5SdPOEy-4WwrpL4jx7hmjpF41omDaLB4pf\/s728-rw-e365\/salesforce.jpg)](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEjOD6ZYqSFsGpRVJxBtltcRoC9-bFtsZJmqFifYX8Igo5yrPbPOmILwcgX3aZrxE52OMY7IzAwCXNy5K_Xd485g9g3SA2KnS0CoIAwsnUkRpDsF4vFlna2kiWl3wy1h2OWm_oFqyi0EIW3qSGaz8K8s0dXRqc5SdPOEy-4WwrpL4jx7hmjpF41omDaLB4pf\/s728-rw-e365\/salesforce.jpg)\nCybersecurity researchers have uncovered over 20 configuration-related risks affecting Salesforce Industry Cloud (aka [Salesforce Industries](https:\/\/www.salesforce.com\/ap\/solutions\/industries\/)), exposing sensitive data to unauthorized internal and external parties.\nThe [weaknesses](https:\/\/appomni.com\/blog\/low-code-high-stakes-salesforce-security\/) affect various components like FlexCards, Data Mappers, Integration Procedures (IProcs), Data Packs, OmniOut, and OmniScript Saved Sessions.\n\"Low-code platforms such as Salesforce Industry Cloud make building applications easier, but that convenience can come at a cost if security isn't prioritized,\" Aaron Costello, chief of SaaS Security Research at AppOmni, said in a statement shared with The Hacker News.\nThese misconfigurations, if left unaddressed, could allow cybercriminals and unauthorized to access encrypted confidential data on employees and customers, session data detailing how users have interacted with Salesforce Industry Cloud, credentials for Salesforce and other company systems, and business logic.\n[![Cybersecurity](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/researchers-uncover-20-configuration.html)](https:\/\/thehackernews.uk\/grc-now-2025-d \"Cybersecurity\")\nFollowing responsible disclosure, Salesforce has addressed three of the shortcomings and issued configuration guidance for another two. The remaining 16 misconfigurations have been left to the customers to fix them on their own.\nThe vulnerabilities that have been assigned CVE identifiers are listed below -\n  * **[CVE-2025-43697](https:\/\/nvd.nist.gov\/view\/vuln\/detail?vulnId=CVE-2025-43697)** (CVSS score: N\/A) - If 'Check Field Level Security' is not enabled for 'Extract' and 'Turbo Extract Data Mappers, the 'View Encrypted Data' permission check is not enforced, exposing cleartext values for the encrypted fields to users with access to a given record\n  * **[CVE-2025-43698](https:\/\/nvd.nist.gov\/view\/vuln\/detail?vulnId=CVE-2025-43698)** (CVSS score: N\/A) - The SOQL data source bypasses any Field-Level Security when fetching data from Salesforce objects\n  * **[CVE-2025-43699](https:\/\/nvd.nist.gov\/view\/vuln\/detail?vulnId=CVE-2025-43699)** (CVSS score: 5.3) - Flexcard does not enforce the 'Required Permissions' field for the OmniUlCard object\n  * **[CVE-2025-43700](https:\/\/nvd.nist.gov\/view\/vuln\/detail?vulnId=CVE-2025-43700)** (CVSS score: 7.5) - Flexcard does not enforce the 'View Encrypted Data' permission, returning plaintext values for data that uses Classic Encryption\n  * **[CVE-2025-43701](https:\/\/nvd.nist.gov\/view\/vuln\/detail?vulnId=CVE-2025-43701)** (CVSS score: 7.5) - FlexCard allows Guest Users to access values for Custom Settings\n\n\nPut simply, attackers can weaponize these issues to bypass security controls and extract sensitive customer or employee information.\nAppOmni said CVE-2025-43967 and CVE-2025-43698 have been tackled through a new security setting called \"\" that customers will have to enable to ensure that only users with the \"View Encrypted Data\" permission may see the plaintext value of fields returned by the Data Mapper.\n[![](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/researchers-uncover-20-configuration.html)](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEhmWgn9kF9KioqBTSlbbcvGrDr4eMmxVLl1B9sdoZEB3nX3-kxA3UGfMWXRvGq4sBXZyH0RpYbKGoryzmDPLR1245SnwxyMuJHkzLWGXFPfqpHCoGoqCQUwPMIsdaLnbfgCu3fbQVZdaWyMwZMeWvpH2MJruTwhZ3nhSMmVxnbfH5OpDt6r7iOH6SJMhtxY\/s728-rw-e365\/app.jpg)\n\"For organizations subject to compliance mandates such as HIPAA, GDPR, SOX, or PCI-DSS, these gaps can represent real regulatory exposure,\" the company said. \"And because it is the customer's responsibility to securely configure these settings, a single missed setting could lead to the breach of thousands of records, with no vendor accountability.\"\nWhen reached for comment, a Salesforce spokesperson told The Hacker News that a vast majority of the issues \"stem from customer configuration issues\" and are not vulnerabilities inherent to the application.\n\"All issues identified in this research have been resolved, with patches made available to customers, and official documentation updated to reflect complete configuration functionality,\" the company said. \"We have not observed any evidence of exploitation in customer environments as a result of these issues.\"\nThe disclosure comes as security researcher Tobia Righi, who goes by the handle MasterSplinter, [disclosed](https:\/\/mastersplinter.work\/research\/salesforce-sqli\/) a Salesforce Object Query Language (SOQL) injection vulnerability that could be exploited to access sensitive user data.\n[![Cybersecurity](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/researchers-uncover-20-configuration.html)](https:\/\/thehackernews.uk\/security-zscaler \"Cybersecurity\")\nThe zero-day vulnerability (no CVE) exists in a default aura controller present in all Salesforce deployments, arising as a result of a user-controlled \"contentDocumentId\" parameter that's unsafely embedded into \"aura:\/\/CsvDataImportResourceFamilyController\/ACTION$getCsvAutoMap\" that creates a pathway for SOQL injection.\nSuccessful exploitation of the flaw could have enabled attackers to insert additional queries through the parameter and extract database contents. The exploit could be further [augmented](https:\/\/github.com\/Splinter0\/mastersplinter\/blob\/main\/content\/research\/salesforce-sqli\/soql-brute.py) by passing a list of IDs correlated to ContentDocument objects that are not public so as to gather information about uploaded documents.\nThe IDs, Righi said, can be generated by means of a [publicly-available brute-force script](https:\/\/github.com\/hypn\/misc-scripts\/blob\/master\/salesforce-id-generator.py) that can generate possible previous or next Salesforce IDs based on a valid input ID. This, in turn, is made possible owing to the fact that Salesforce IDs do not actually provide a security boundary and are actually somewhat predictable.\n\"As noted in the research, after receiving the report, our security team promptly investigated and resolved the issue. We have not observed any evidence of exploitation in customer environments,\" the Salesforce spokesperson said. \"We appreciate Tobia's efforts to responsibly disclose this issue to Salesforce, and we continue to encourage the security research community to report potential issues through our established channels.\"\nFound this article interesting? Follow us on and to read more exclusive content we post.\nSHARE [_\uf1e0_](javascript:void\\(0\\))\n_\uf1e0_ Share\n[_\ue80a_](javascript:void\\(0\\)) [_\uf1d4_ Share on Hacker News](https:\/\/news.ycombinator.com\/submitlink?u=https%3A%2F%2Fthehackernews.com%2F2025%2F06%2Fresearchers-uncover-20-configuration.html&t=Researchers%20Uncover%2020%2B%20Configuration%20Risks%2C%20Including%20Five%20CVEs%2C%20in%20Salesforce%20Industry%20Cloud) _\uf0e0_ Share on Email [_\uf232_ Share on WhatsApp](https:\/\/api.whatsapp.com\/send?text=Researchers%20Uncover%2020%2B%20Configuration%20Risks%2C%20Including%20Five%20CVEs%2C%20in%20Salesforce%20Industry%20Cloud \u2014 https%3A%2F%2Fthehackernews.com%2F2025%2F06%2Fresearchers-uncover-20-configuration.html) [![Facebook Messenger](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/researchers-uncover-20-configuration.html)Share on Facebook Messenger](fb-messenger:\/\/share\/?link=https%3A%2F%2Fthehackernews.com%2F2025%2F06%2Fresearchers-uncover-20-configuration.html&app_id=280117418781535) [_\uf2c6_ Share on Telegram](https:\/\/telegram.me\/share\/url?url=https%3A%2F%2Fthehackernews.com%2F2025%2F06%2Fresearchers-uncover-20-configuration.html&text=Researchers%20Uncover%2020%2B%20Configuration%20Risks%2C%20Including%20Five%20CVEs%2C%20in%20Salesforce%20Industry%20Cloud)\n[SHARE _\uf1e0_](javascript:void\\(0\\))\n[AppOmni](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/search\/label\/AppOmni)[Cloud security](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/search\/label\/Cloud%20security)[Compliance](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/search\/label\/