"markdown": "[_\ue80a_](javascript:void\\(0\\))\nContact\/Tip Us\n[ _\uf0e0_ Reach out to get featured\u2014contact us to send your exclusive story idea, research, hacks, or ask us a question or leave a comment\/feedback! ](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/p\/submit-news.html)\nFollow Us On Social Media\n[_\uf167_](https:\/\/www.youtube.com\/c\/thehackernews?sub_confirmation=1)\n[_\uf09e_ RSS Feeds](https:\/\/feeds.feedburner.com\/TheHackersNews) [_\uf0f3_ Email Alerts](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/rust-based-myth-stealer-malware-spread.html#email-outer) [_\uf2c6_ Telegram Channel](https:\/\/t.me\/+wKrQxniFO1Q1OTRk)\n[![Maximizing Efficiency and Security in Government Cloud Environments](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEjmy-PEKftk11FkFxOmdctMAQvuNZs31RGGDpxe66f8A72_iHu6NGjK0aDPU80YpR1m3Yo1zLbr-jR6RpBlz7xcjBXRasIVB2ELtOxV39r54fCWfLtySFA6x5eeDix9sZtk4aXcEBSQaEqR-y1EY_wtaQI34gSbWHYLpRqj9oUOE12EE2S3xGVTsoU9TIZ8\/s728-e100\/gov-cloud-efficiency-d.png)](https:\/\/thehackernews.uk\/gov-cloud-efficiency-d)\n# [Rust-based Myth Stealer Malware Spread via Fake Gaming Sites Targets Chrome, Firefox Users](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/rust-based-myth-stealer-malware-spread.html)\n_\ue802_ Jun 10, 2025 _\ue804_ Ravie LakshmananCybersecurity \/ Malware\n[![Fake Gaming Sites Targets Chrome, Firefox Users](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEjRKwiZnsYHGtJp-sleouvb1dStV2oqwiIJaaOFg40y3Cqk-yer3dAXAe83OxeAZc0H6PuwlB7diwhxgzU4cklYeRlr_IL9yM2Gpuj7UxMfHoNCo4vPAkUEXNOcfSJBajXM7hAP4O0U9paQrd82xcaX3mTr69I3CjwoiVaZ_jDC8W7DluC27WO_SC0CkVmW\/s728-rw-e365\/gaming.jpg)](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEjRKwiZnsYHGtJp-sleouvb1dStV2oqwiIJaaOFg40y3Cqk-yer3dAXAe83OxeAZc0H6PuwlB7diwhxgzU4cklYeRlr_IL9yM2Gpuj7UxMfHoNCo4vPAkUEXNOcfSJBajXM7hAP4O0U9paQrd82xcaX3mTr69I3CjwoiVaZ_jDC8W7DluC27WO_SC0CkVmW\/s728-rw-e365\/gaming.jpg)\nCybersecurity researchers have shed light on a previously undocumented Rust-based information stealer called Myth Stealer that's being propagated via fraudulent gaming websites.\n\"Upon execution, the malware displays a fake window to appear legitimate while simultaneously decrypting and executing malicious code in the background,\" Trellix security researchers Niranjan Hegde, Vasantha Lakshmanan Ambasankar, and Adarsh S [said](https:\/\/www.trellix.com\/en-in\/blogs\/research\/demystifying-myth-stealer-a-rust-based-infostealer\/) in an analysis.\nThe stealer, initially marketed on Telegram for free under beta in late December 2024, has since transitioned to a malware-as-a-service (MaaS) model. It's equipped to steal passwords, cookies, and autofill information from both Chromium- and Gecko-based browsers, such as Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi, and Mozilla Firefox.\nThe operators of the malware have been found maintaining a number of Telegram channels to advertise the sale of compromised accounts as well as provide testimonials of their service. These channels have been shut down by Telegram.\nEvidence shows that Myth Stealer is distributed through fake websites, including one hosted on Google's Blogger, offering various video games under the pretext of testing them. It's worth noting that a near-identical Blogger page has been used to deliver another stealer malware known as [AgeoStealer](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/05\/weekly-recap-zero-day-exploits.html#:~:text=AgeoStealer%20Stealer%20Disguises%20as%20a%20Video%20Game), as disclosed by Flashpoint in April 2025.\nTrellix said it also discovered the malware being distributed as a cracked version of a game cheating software called DDrace in an online forum, highlighting the myriad distribution vehicles.\n[![Cybersecurity](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/rust-based-myth-stealer-malware-spread.html)](https:\/\/thehackernews.uk\/grc-now-2025-d \"Cybersecurity\")\nRegardless of the initial access vector, the downloaded loader displays a fake setup window to the user to deceive them into thinking that a legitimate application is executed. In the background, the loader decrypts and launches the stealer component.\nIn a 64-bit DLL file, the stealer attempts to terminate running processes associated with various web browsers before stealing the data and exfiltrating it to a remote server, or, in some cases, to a Discord webhook.\n\"It also contains anti-analysis techniques such as string obfuscation and system checks using filenames and usernames,\" the researchers said. \"The malware authors regularly update stealer code to evade AV detection and introduce additional functionality such as screen capture capability and clipboard hijacking.\"\n[![](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/rust-based-myth-stealer-malware-spread.html)](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEjbSNAhp0REWg_e5GzO7Ss2qg2KuWByQba8XpL3710LEgsaLlp51-7rlu6D_gJZCc4xMoulBK4bTbyqDQ89QOJXEsg3ss492IB8CzjtACBVuimoLYs20YHHXP67MxpbbfiffgxxhRFSyeFQXxhD56DiXqJKfJXp-jyfBGk85tTjfb2FdmhsVqHsNEsHNGXU\/s728-rw-e365\/games.jpg)\nMyth Stealer is by no means alone when it comes to using game cheat lures to distribute malware. Last week, Palo Alto Networks Unit 42 shed light on another Windows malware referred to as Blitz that's spread through backdoored game cheats and cracked installers for legitimate programs.\nPrimarily propagated via an attacker-controlled Telegram channel, Blitz consists of two stages: A downloader that's responsible for a bot payload, which is designed to log keystrokes, take screenshots, download\/upload files, and inject code. It also comes fitted with a denial-of-service (DoS) function against web servers and drops an XMRig miner.\nThe backdoored cheat performs anti-sandbox checks before retrieving the malware's next stage, with the downloader only running when the victim logs in again after logging out or a reboot. The downloader is also configured to run the same anti-sandbox checks prior to dropping the bot payload.\nWhat's notable about the attack chain is that the Blitz bot and XMR cryptocurrency miner payloads, along with components of its command-and-control (C2) infrastructure, are hosted in a Hugging Face Space. Hugging Face has locked the user account following responsible disclosure.\nAs of late April 2025, Blitz is estimated to have amassed 289 infections in 26 countries, led by Russia, Ukraine, Belarus, and Kazakhstan. Last month, the threat actor behind Blitz claimed on their Telegram channel that they are hanging up the boots after they apparently found that the cheat had a trojan embedded in it. They also provided a removal tool to wipe the malware from victim systems.\n\"The person behind Blitz malware appears to be a Russian speaker who uses the moniker sw1zzx on social media platforms,\" Unit 42 [said](https:\/\/unit42.paloaltonetworks.com\/blitz-malware-2025\/). \"This malware operator is likely the developer of Blitz.\" \nThe development comes as CYFIRMA detailed a new C#-based remote access trojan (RAT) named DuplexSpy RAT that comes with extensive capabilities for surveillance, persistence, and system control. It was [published](https:\/\/github.com\/iss4cf0ng\/DuplexSpyCS) on GitHub in April 2025, claiming it's intended for \"educational and ethical demonstration only.\"\n[![](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/rust-based-myth-stealer-malware-spread.html)](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEj-ZfRyvQyVob4qKKkZlc19D65jv0k1tfWmfIsw4EHZlJLr0N5YQVidXj5-mz7VFW0YI1AnV_4ob0C7uT3aPquec6-1tftgVtabpzvfsoJhlgzjgDci9KhqJfA-vtryQEJGgFww2H2mtYAU9yrp7SkwXXUtB01Be0yIYAMFhCLBfFSYX1kGf5efBljvNbyh\/s728-rw-e365\/Blitz.png)  \n---  \nBlitz infection chain  \n\"It establishes persistence via startup folder replication and Windows registry modifications while employing fileless execution and privilege escalation techniques for stealth,\" the company [said](https:\/\/www.cyfirma.com\/research\/duplexspy-rat-stealthy-windows-malware-enabling-full-remote-control-and-surveillance\/). \"Key features include keylogging, screen capture, webcam\/audio spying, remote shell, and anti-analysis functions.\"\nBesides featuring the ability to remotely play audio or system sounds on the victim's machine, DuplexSpy RAT incorporates a power control module that makes it possible for the attacker to remotely execute system-level commands on the compromised host, such as shutdown, restart, logout, and sleep.\n[![Cybersecurity](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/rust-based-myth-stealer-malware-spread.html)](https:\/\/thehackernews.uk\/security-zscaler \"Cybersecurity\")\n\"[The malware] enforces a fake lock screen by displaying an attacker-supplied image (Base64-encoded) in full screen while disabling user interaction,\" CYFIRMA added. \"It prevents closure unless explicitly permitted, simulating a system freeze or ransom notice to manipulate or extort the victim.\"\nThe findings also follow a report from Positive Technologies that [multiple threat actors](https:\/\/global.ptsecurity.com\/analytics\/pt-esc-threat-intelligence\/crypters-and-tools-part-2-different-paws-same-tangle), including [TA558](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2024\/04\/ta558-hackers-weaponize-images-for-wide.html), [Blind Eagle](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/03\/blind-eagle-hacks-colombian.html), [Aggah](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2023\/06\/chinese-postalfurious-gang-strikes-uae.html) (aka [Hagga](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2023\/05\/xworm-malware-exploits-follina.html)), [PhaseShifters](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/02\/sticky-werewolf-uses-undocumented.html) (aka Angry Likho, Sticky Werewolf, and UAC-0050), [UAC-0050](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2024\/01\/uac-0050-group-using-new-phishing.html), and [PhantomControl](https:\/\/www.esentire.com\/blog\/phantomcontrol-returns-with-ande-loader-and-swaetrat), are using a crypter-as-a-service offering called [Crypters And Tools](https:\/\/global.ptsecurity.com\/analytics\/pt-esc-threat-intelligence\/crypters-and-tools-one-tool-for-thousands-of-malicious-files) to obfuscate files like Ande Loader.\nAttack chains using Crypters And Tools have targeted the United States, Eastern Europe (including Russia), and Latin America. One platform where the crypter is sold is nitrosoftwares[.]com, which also offers various tools, including exploits, crypters, loggers, and cryptocurrency clippers, among others.\nFound this article interesting? Follow us on and to read more exclusive content we post.\nSHARE [_\uf09a_](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/rust-based-myth-stealer-malware-spread.html#link_share) [_\uf099_](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/rust-based-myth-stealer-malware-spread.html#link_share) [_\uf0e1_](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/rust-based-myth-stealer-malware-spread.html#link_share) [_\uf1e0_](javascript:void\\(0\\))\n[_\uf099_ Tweet](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/rust-based-myth-stealer-malware-spread.html#link_share)\n[_\uf0e1_ Share](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/rust-based-myth-stealer-malware-spread.html#link_share)\n[_\uf09a_ Share](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/rust-based-myth-stealer-malware-spread.html#link_share)\n_\uf1e0_ Share\n[_\ue80a_](javascript:void\\(0\\)) [_\uf09a_ Share on Facebook](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/rust-based-myth-stealer-malware-spread.html#link_share) [_\uf099_ Share on Twitter](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/rust-based-myth-stealer-malware-spread.html#link_share) [_\uf0e1_ Share on Linkedin](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/rust-based-myth-stealer-malware-spread.html#link_share) [_\uf281_ Share on Reddit](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/rust-based-myth-stealer-malware-spread.html#link_share) [_\uf1d4_ Share on Hacker News](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/rust-based-myth-stealer-malware-spread.html#link_share) [_\uf0e0_ Share on Email](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/rust-based-myth-stealer-malware-spread.html#link_share) [_\uf23