"markdown": "[_\ue80a_](javascript:void\\(0\\))\nContact\/Tip Us\n[ _\uf0e0_ Reach out to get featured\u2014contact us to send your exclusive story idea, research, hacks, or ask us a question or leave a comment\/feedback! ](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/p\/submit-news.html)\nFollow Us On Social Media\n[_\uf167_](https:\/\/www.youtube.com\/c\/thehackernews?sub_confirmation=1)\n[_\uf09e_ RSS Feeds](https:\/\/feeds.feedburner.com\/TheHackersNews) [_\uf0f3_ Email Alerts](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/how-to-build-lean-security-model-5.html#email-outer) [_\uf2c6_ Telegram Channel](https:\/\/t.me\/+wKrQxniFO1Q1OTRk)\n[![Maximizing Efficiency and Security in Government Cloud Environments](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEjmy-PEKftk11FkFxOmdctMAQvuNZs31RGGDpxe66f8A72_iHu6NGjK0aDPU80YpR1m3Yo1zLbr-jR6RpBlz7xcjBXRasIVB2ELtOxV39r54fCWfLtySFA6x5eeDix9sZtk4aXcEBSQaEqR-y1EY_wtaQI34gSbWHYLpRqj9oUOE12EE2S3xGVTsoU9TIZ8\/s728-e100\/gov-cloud-efficiency-d.png)](https:\/\/thehackernews.uk\/gov-cloud-efficiency-d)\n# [How to Build a Lean Security Model: 5 Lessons from River Island](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/how-to-build-lean-security-model-5.html)\n_\ue802_ Jun 11, 2025 _\ue804_ The Hacker NewsVulnerability Management \/ Cyber Hygiene\n[![](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEh6wSpt91CT1tPHGjbJo-GWogAD8urOihP9hnqOQqrIwScgnM5QBbQZ7-tAeh-M8UFsAsbAL9DqQVEOaiQvJs8EI_YGLlI-R-j4-zFGj5xOqffhtLS3tWozV-YvJl4OuKZnoZVrKNxh8udOa6uYCUjak1KzL1D0gO0imNdEUuLgAzIF8h_h75Kw_e_G8KM\/s728-rw-e365\/lean-security-modal.jpg)](https:\/\/blogger.googleusercontent.com\/img\/b\/R29vZ2xl\/AVvXsEh6wSpt91CT1tPHGjbJo-GWogAD8urOihP9hnqOQqrIwScgnM5QBbQZ7-tAeh-M8UFsAsbAL9DqQVEOaiQvJs8EI_YGLlI-R-j4-zFGj5xOqffhtLS3tWozV-YvJl4OuKZnoZVrKNxh8udOa6uYCUjak1KzL1D0gO0imNdEUuLgAzIF8h_h75Kw_e_G8KM\/s728-rw-e365\/lean-security-modal.jpg)\nIn today\u2019s security landscape, budgets are tight, attack surfaces are sprawling, and new threats emerge daily. Maintaining a strong security posture under these circumstances without a large team or budget can be a real challenge. Yet lean security models are not only possible - they can be highly effective.\nRiver Island, one of the UK\u2019s leading fashion retailers, offers a powerful case study on how to do more with less. As River Island\u2019s InfoSec Officer, Sunil Patel and his small team of three are responsible for securing over 200 stores, an e-commerce platform, a major distribution center, and head offices. With no headcount growth on the horizon, Sunil had to rethink how security could scale effectively.\nBy adopting a lean security model, powered by [Intruder\u2019s exposure management platform](https:\/\/www.intruder.io\/), the team was able to improve visibility, respond faster to threats, and empower others across the business to fix what matters most.\nHere are five key lessons from their approach that any security team can apply.\n## 1. Automate Attack Surface Visibility[#](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/how-to-build-lean-security-model-5.html#1-automate-attack-surface-visibility)\nA lean security model relies on the ability to quickly and clearly understand your external attack surface. River Island\u2019s team lacked a central way to track what was exposed to the internet. Without a single, up-to-date view of their internet-facing assets, they relied on spreadsheets and manual checks and struggled to keep up with new risks stemming from a constantly changing infrastructure.\nBy adopting [continuous network monitoring](https:\/\/www.intruder.io\/attack-surface-management?utm_source=thehackernews&utm_medium=p_referral&utm_campaign=global%7Cfixed%7C11_06_25_river_island) as part of their exposure management process, the team now detects attack surface changes automatically. When a new or unexpected service - like a login page, admin panel, or database - becomes accessible from the internet, they\u2019re notified in real-time. This gives Sunil and his team a live, accurate view of what\u2019s exposed and makes it easy to start automatically scanning these exposed assets for vulnerabilities.\n## 2. Select the Right Tools for the Job[#](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/how-to-build-lean-security-model-5.html#2-select-the-right-tools-for-the-job)\nThe last thing a lean team needs is a stack of overlapping tools - each doing little, none doing enough.\nRiver Island had a range of security solutions in place, but many were underutilized. Sunil estimated they were \u201conly getting about 5-6% of the possible value\u201d from some products.\nRather than adding more to the mix, the team consolidated. This means less time spent context-switching and more time acting on clear, unified insights. With a smaller toolkit, it is easier to build the integrations and automation that are an essential part of being lean.\n## 3. Automate Emerging Threat Detection[#](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/how-to-build-lean-security-model-5.html#3-automate-emerging-threat-detection)\nHigh-profile vulnerabilities like Log4j put lean teams under immense pressure. When critical vulnerabilities emerge, your ability to stay secure depends on how quickly you can assess exposure. But with limited resources, scrambling to do this manually is inefficient and unsustainable.\nUnified exposure management platforms like Intruder take the pressure off by automatically scanning for newly disclosed critical vulnerabilities so that you\u2019re not left waiting for your next weekly or monthly scan to find out whether you have an issue. \nSpeaking to the impact of this, Sunil said, \u201cWhen Log4j hit, our CIO asked if we were affected. I could tell him straight away: \u2018We\u2019re good - Intruder\u2019s scanned for it and we\u2019re in the clear.\u2019\u201d\nThis level of assurance builds trust with leadership, avoids unnecessary fire drills, and frees up the team to focus on remediation rather than investigation.\n## 4. Enable Asset Owners to Fix Issues Fast[#](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/how-to-build-lean-security-model-5.html#4-enable-asset-owners-to-fix-issues-fast)\nIn adopting a lean security model, the goal isn\u2019t to fix everything yourself - it\u2019s to make sure the right people are equipped to fix the right things, fast. That means removing the security team as a bottleneck and empowering others to remediate weaknesses.\n\u201cOne of my goals was to take the security team out of the equation completely from a process perspective,\u201d said Sunil.\nPreviously, the InfoSec team was responsible for chasing down asset owners and translating technical recommendations for non-security experts. Now, by integrating their exposure management platform with Jira, vulnerabilities are routed directly to the relevant teams - along with easy-to-follow instructions needed to take action.\nThis shift has freed up InfoSec to focus on higher priorities, while service delivery managers handle day-to-day remediation.\nSunil said, \u201cWe\u2019re not the nagging manager anymore. We just monitor and make sure things are progressing.\u201d\n## 5. Report on Cyber Hygiene[#](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/how-to-build-lean-security-model-5.html#5-report-on-cyber-hygiene)\nWhen you're running a lean security team, the last thing you want is to spend your limited time manually pulling reports or communicating updates to stakeholders. But visibility still matters - especially at the leadership level.\nAt River Island, that trust was built by shifting away from ad-hoc reporting towards automated dashboards that clearly show what\u2019s exposed, what\u2019s been fixed, and what still needs attention.\nSunil said, \u201cI told my CIO, \u2018You don\u2019t have many one-to-ones with me,\u2019 and he laughed and said, \u2018That\u2019s a good thing - it means nothing\u2019s broken. Intruder gives him the confidence that we\u2019ve got it covered, so he doesn\u2019t need to check-in. That\u2019s how I know things are working.\u201d\n## Small Teams, Big Impact[#](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/how-to-build-lean-security-model-5.html#small-teams-big-impact)\nBeing lean doesn\u2019t mean being underpowered. With the right tools, processes, and mindset, security teams of any size can build scalable, resilient, and efficient operations. River Island\u2019s experience shows that doing more with less isn\u2019t just possible - it can be a smarter, more sustainable approach to security.\n**Under pressure to do more with less?[Try Intruder for free with a 14-day trial](https:\/\/portal.intruder.io\/free_trial?utm_source=thehackernews&utm_medium=p_referral&utm_campaign=global%7Cfixed%7C11_06_25_river_island).**\nFound this article interesting? This article is a contributed piece from one of our valued partners. Follow us on and to read more exclusive content we post.\nSHARE [_\uf1e0_](javascript:void\\(0\\))\n_\uf1e0_ Share\n[_\ue80a_](javascript:void\\(0\\)) [_\uf1d4_ Share on Hacker News](https:\/\/news.ycombinator.com\/submitlink?u=https%3A%2F%2Fthehackernews.com%2F2025%2F06%2Fhow-to-build-lean-security-model-5.html&t=How%20to%20Build%20a%20Lean%20Security%20Model%3A%205%20Lessons%20from%20River%20Island) _\uf0e0_ Share on Email [_\uf232_ Share on WhatsApp](https:\/\/api.whatsapp.com\/send?text=How%20to%20Build%20a%20Lean%20Security%20Model%3A%205%20Lessons%20from%20River%20Island \u2014 https%3A%2F%2Fthehackernews.com%2F2025%2F06%2Fhow-to-build-lean-security-model-5.html) [![Facebook Messenger](https:\/\/thehackernews.com\/2025\/06\/how-to-build-lean-security-model-5.html)Share on Facebook Messenger](fb-messenger:\/\/share\/?link=https%3A%2F%2Fthehackernews.com%2F2025%2F06%2Fhow-to-build-lean-security-model-5.html&app_id=280117418781535) [_\uf2c6_ Share on Telegram](https:\/\/telegram.me\/share\/url?url=https%3A%2F%2Fthehackernews.com%2F2025%2F06%2Fhow-to-build-lean-security-model-5.html&text=How%20to%20Build%20a%20Lean%20Security%20Model%3A%205%20Lessons%