"title": "SparkKitty, SparkCat\u2019s little brother: A new Trojan spy found in the App Store and Google Play",,
"url": "https:\/\/securelist.com\/sparkkitty-ios-android-malware\/116793\/",,
"summary": "In January 2025, a new spyware campaign named SparkKitty was discovered targeting iOS and Android devices. SparkKitty is linked to the previous SparkCat spyware campaign, specifically targeting cryptocurrency assets by stealing images containing sensitive information such as seed phrases for crypto wallets. It infiltrated official app stores, iOS App Store and Google Play, affecting both platforms. For iOS, the malware was distributed via malicious frameworks and libraries disguised as legitimate components. On Android, the malware was delivered as a Trojan in Java and Kotlin, with the Kotlin version being a malicious Xposed module. The campaign has been active since February 2024. SparkKitty is a newly discovered Trojan that has been found in apps on the App Store and Google Play. The primary functionality of this malware involves stealing images from infected devices. The malware has been embedded in various apps, including casino apps and a messaging app with crypto exchange features. These apps exploit frameworks such as LSPosed and malicious Xposed modules to execute its payload. The malware interacts with command-and-control (C2) servers to exfiltrate images and device information and uses modified cryptographic libraries. Google and Apple have been notified to remove the infected apps from their platforms. The article discusses the newly discovered malware 'SparkKitty,' a Trojan spy found in both the Apple App Store and Google Play. The spyware doesn't seem directly focused on victims' cryptocurrency assets like SparkCat but is believed to steal photos to potentially capture crypto wallet seed phrases. The malware is primarily targeting users in Southeast Asia and China through infected applications downloaded from unofficial sources and legitimate app stores. This operation is linked to the SparkCat campaign, as there are shared frameworks and paths observed in both malware. Victims may include those using crypto-themed apps and various Chinese gambling, TikTok, and adult games.",,
